{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"I’ll read the packet and judging rules, then assess all 25 answers only against the evidence each request shows."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md && cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/packet-B.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md && cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/packet-B.json'","aggregated_output":"# 判断的尺子，第 1 版（D3 立场金标）\n\n写于 2026-11-02。Alex 的话：\"你已经改了好几次尺子了，到底什么是对的，以及你怎么 freeze。\"\n\n## 0. 一句话\n\n一道题判得对不对，拿它和**一位资深对冲基金分析师读完同样 14 天材料后的看法**比：他认为市场原来预期什么、材料推出来的结果是多少、\n相对预期是高是低；系统最后一次判断落的那一档，有没有把这个结果包进去。分析师不是我，不是写代码的人，不看系统的输出；他的看法写成文件、算出\nsha256、记进决定，之后一个字不改。\n\n## 1. 什么是\"对\"\n\n- **单位**：一道金标题 × 一个时点（14 天窗口末，2026-07-14 收盘后）。23 道题：22 道种子（`poc/seeds/nvda-2026-07-01-v2.json` v1–v22，每次运行里\n  编号固定）加金标 must_open 里种子没覆盖的 1 道（俄亥俄租约担保是/否，2026-07-01 的 must_open）。其余 27 条 must_open 都是这 23 道的同一赌注\n  （信用支持金额 ×10、Kyber 出货 ×11、云厂商资本开支 ×5、内存价格 ×1、中国放行 H200 ×2），映射写在 `questions-v1.json` 里，Alex 签。\n- **材料**：签过的抽取标准对这 14 天的读法，即金标 golden-v5（dev，118 篇）的 309 条事项（核心 86、辅助 151、可选 72），带日期、标题、\n  来源层、说话人、原文和意思。不用系统的账本（账本随每臂变，尺子不能随臂变），不用原始全文（那是重做抽取）。这和抽取的尺子是同一把：标准第 5 节\n  说的\"分析员会记的\"就是这些。\n- **分析师写什么**（每道题一条，`reader-brief.md` 是他拿到的全部说明，里面只有标准和 PRD 06 的原话，没有系统的提示词）：\n  1. `reference`：到 2026-07-14 为止市场预期这道题的答案是什么（一致预期、公司指引、既定计划或截止日），出自哪几条事项；材料里没有就写\n     \"没有，参照 = 现状\"。\n  2. `implied_outcome`：材料推出来的结果——数字题给一个数或一个区间，事件题给是/否和日期——怎么推的，出自哪几条。\n  3. `direction`：结果相对参照：above / in_line / below；事件题 yes / no。\n  4. `confidence`：high / medium / low（材料把这件事定到什么程度）。\n  5. `decisive_items`：撑起这个看法的 2–6 条事项编号；`against`：最强的反面一条。\n  6. `no_view`：材料里没有任何关于这道题的东西时为 true；这道题不进分数，记\"没练到\"。\n  7. `reasoning`：三到六句。只用材料，不用材料外的知识，每个说法都要有编号。\n- **分析师是谁**：两位互相独立、都没写过这套代码也没看过系统输出的模型读者（A：Codex；B：一个干净上下文的 Claude 读者），各自从同一个包\n  写一遍。都标明\"模型写的\"。**Alex 定（2026-11-02，D262）：金标 = 读者 B 的 23 条答案，原样；读者 A 的答案留作对照记录**，两者的差别\n  （区间或是/否不同 3/23：q09、q12、q23；方向标签不同 12/20）就是这把尺子自身噪声的记录（`reconcile-v1.md`）。\n- **判\"对\"的方法**（`score_judgement.py`，全部是代码）：取一次运行里每道题在 2026-07-14 及之前最后一次判断。数字题：金标的区间\n  [low, high] 和系统所选那一档的 [lo, hi) 有没有交集，有 = 一致；事件题：是/否相同 = 一致。没判过的题单独数（那是证据没到，D2 的事，不算\n  判断错）。另外打印两条零假设：**查表基线**（把金标写的参照数落进哪一档，就选那一档）和**永远中性基线**；判断要在这两条线之上才算有判断力。\n  读者之间的分歧率一起打印，那是尺子自身的噪声。\n- **第二层，读不是数**：分数之外，对一个样本的判断，由没写代码的读者看系统写的理由（从 judging-v6 起还有 reference 和 implied_outcome）\n  是不是从所示证据得出。这是 AGENTS §7.2 的\"分数是线索、trace 是测量\"。\n\n## 2. 怎么 freeze\n\n原则先于机制（D261，Alex 2026-11-02：\"我们做完的 review 过的尺子金标不能再动了\"）：这一页、读者说明、题单和金标在 Alex 审过签字之后一个字不改；之前只在建的过程中改，签字前不拿它打任何分。下面是把这句话变成代码和文件的做法。\n\n1. 读者只看 `packet-v1.json` 和 `reader-brief.md`，看不到任何运行、账本、提示词；读者的原始答案 `reader-A.json`、`reader-B.json` 原样保存，不改。\n2. 我不写金标。我做的只有：拼包（代码）、按 direction 机械对齐两位读者、把分歧按材料写成对照记录（引编号）；分歧由材料定，定不了的列给 Alex。\n3. 金标文件 `gold-v1.json` 带 `ruler_version: 1`、两位读者的 sha256、每道题的来源（A/B 一致，或裁定及理由）；文件的 sha256 写进\n   `gold-v1.sha256` 和决定 D261。Alex 签的是：23 道题的清单和映射、裁定过的题。签之前每个分数都标\"金标第 1 版，未签\"。\n4. `score_judgement.py` 开头读金标的 sha256 和 `ruler_version`，和自己写死的对不上就拒绝打分，打印两边的 sha；每份分数都印金标版本。\n5. 冻住后不改文件。发现错了，写进 `corrections-v1.json` 一条带日期的记录：哪道题、改前、改后、为什么；打分器不悄悄应用它，只在分数旁印\n   \"有 N 条更正未并入\"。更正积累到要并入时，出第 2 版：新文件、新 sha、决定里记，旧分数作废并说明。\n6. 这一页的定义本身改了（单位、材料、字段、判对的方法），就是第 2 版尺子：金标必须重写，打分器拒绝版本不符的金标。改定义只能由记录在 Alex\n   原话上的决定触发（AGENTS §7.1）。\n7. 一把尺子：判断这一步不再有别的评分标准。我昨天定的判断规则 D260 是系统这边的规则，不是尺子；读者的说明里没有它。\n\n**冻结记录**：2026-11-02，`gold-v1.json` sha256 `636c056d1e4252fa759660803067e6ccd67a81067b012905e918aa9dec4005f2`，写在 `gold-v1.sha256` 和 `score_judgement.py` 里；`corrections-v1.json` 为空。当天下午提过的两条改法（读者分叉时打三档分；五五开的题不计分）Alex 没采纳：一题一个区间，每题都算。\n\n这把尺子作为整体（抽取标准 + 题目 + 判断）写在 AGENTS §7.1 \"The frozen ruler\"，每个文件的 sha256 由 `tests/test_frozen_rulers.py` 守着：任何一个动了，整套测试变红。\n\n## 2′. 这把尺子沿用了什么，新的只有哪一层\n\n沿用、不重做：抽取标准页（第 3 版签字 D243 + D250 的四条界线）和 golden-v5 是这把尺子的**材料**，按 sha 引用（`packet-v1.json` 记了金标的 sha）；golden-v5 的题目部分（28 条 must_open，题目标准第 3 版）映射进 23 道题；种子 v2（D253）给题的身份和档的边界；PRD 06 的判断规则和 AGENTS §8 的分析师原话是读者说明里的全部规则；桌面尺子 README 的 D1、D2 不动。2026-10-27 的步骤 3/4 金标（`research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/`，24 例，量的是重复 / 替换 / 并入的决定，不是档位）里写的分析师标准 A（谁先说的，复述只加一个出处不加事实）、B（期间和定义要对上，季度不是全年）和这份说明一致，两者量的是不同步骤；那份金标是在旧账本上写的，助手自己写的，当时就标了不是独立金标。新的只有一层：判断这一步的档位从来没有金标，这是第一份，还有打分器。\n\n## 3. 这把尺子不量什么\n\n该开的题开了没有（D1）、证据挂得对不对（D2）、14 天里每天的路径、依赖和主题推力。它只量：窗口末，系统的档位有没有包住分析师认为的结果。\n\n## 4. 已知的弱点，写明\n\n- 读者是模型，不是人。金标是一位读者（B）的看法，Alex 选的；另一位（A）留作对照，两者在 3/23 道题上区间不交，这是尺子的噪声下限；Alex 的裁定是人的那一票。\n- 材料外的知识：事后结果（FY2027 年报、2026 年底 Rubin 出不出货）都在模型知识截止之后，无法作弊；但模型可能用它记得的\"常识\"补材料，说明里\n  禁止并要求每句引编号，对照时查。\n- 参照：材料里不一定有每道题的一致预期（内存价格、CoWoS 产能），那时参照是现状，direction 的意义弱一点，分数仍按区间交集算。\n- 种子的档不是围着一致预期写的（总收入题一致预期 $392B 落在中性档 350–400 的上沿）：所以分数不比\"看涨/看跌\"的标签，只比区间有没有包住。\n\n## 5. 文件\n\n`judgement-gold/`：`ruler-v1.md`（本页）、`reader-brief.md`、`build_packet.py` → `packet-v1.json` + `questions-v1.json`、\n`run_reader_codex.py` → `reader-A.json`、读者 B 的答案 `reader-B.json`、`reconcile.py` → `gold-v1.json` + `reconcile-v1.md`、\n`gold-v1.sha256`、`corrections-v1.json`、`score_judgement.py`。\n[\n {\n  \"item\": 1,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The amount of signed credit-support, guarantee, or similar credit-enhancement commitments NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners under the new model.\\\", FY2028, due 2027-12-31): What total amount in USD of credit-support, guarantee, or similar credit-enhancement commitments will NVIDIA have signed for AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model by 2027-12-31, as established by NVIDIA’s filings or signed partner agreements?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 100,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 100,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 1,000,000,000 USD to below 5,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 10,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 10,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 27 items]\\n[Not shown: 16 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e1 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced a new business model in which AI clouds can procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers under a revenue-sharing and credit-support arrangement. NVIDIA said the AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning standard product revenue plus a share of cloud revenue on the supported capacity, and that this provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … To address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players. … This new model enables AI c\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13, e14, e20, e23, e30, e36, e61, e78, e87, e143, e191, e247; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation blog; original source Firmus Technologies ; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] Firmus Technologies is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs. Firmus co-CEO Tim Rosenfield said the company is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory to help more customers access compute.\\n    Quote: Firmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs. … “AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15, e25, e28, e31, e39, e63, e80, e89, e111, e145; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e13 [rumour; The Information; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] The Information reported on July 1 that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement; an NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the program's existence.\\n    Quote: On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. … In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud provider\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e16 [rumour; The Information; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\n    Quote: According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\n- e17 [fact; moomoo.com (citing NVIDIA's regulatory filing); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to a regulatory filing NVIDIA submitted in May 2026 (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\\n    Quote: According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\\n- e24 [fact; qz.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] Nvidia named Sharon AI as one of two initial partners in its revenue-sharing program; Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: Nvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27, e62, e79, e88, e110, e144, e248; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e25 [fact; qz.com; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] Nvidia named Firmus Technologies as one of two initial partners in its revenue-sharing program; a campus Firmus is developing in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Quote: A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e30 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com (Georgia Butler); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Nvidia is acting as a financial backstop for neocloud customers purchasing its AI chips, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of their revenue. In a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said the model lets AI clouds procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through 'economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model'; under the partnership, AI clouds sell Nvidia-powered cloud services and Nvidia earns both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, which Nvidia says gives it a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream. No dollar amount of guarantee or credit exposure is disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. … In a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said: \\\"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, a\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e31 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com (Georgia Butler); original source Firmus and Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted Nvidia's backstop offer: Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs. No guarantee amount or contract value is disclosed.\\n    Quote: Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\\nFirmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e36 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new \\\"revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress: partner AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, and NVIDIA collects its usual hardware product revenue plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity, which the post described as a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream. Neither NVIDIA nor its partners disclosed the revenue-split percentages. Australia's Sharon AI and Singapore-based Firmus Technologies were named the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs. Per Bloomberg, developers receive token credits in exchange for a slice of their future sales.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs. … Nvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. … In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitch\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e37 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed in an 8-K filing dated June 12 that its agreement with NVIDIA runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to NVIDIA's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n- e38 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, so portions of its income are pledged in two directions.\\n    Quote: The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\\n- e78 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Nvidia 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] On July 1, 2026, Nvidia unveiled a financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which it acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments—guaranteeing a floor utilization rate and committing to rent back any idle capacity at a predetermined fixed rate—in exchange for a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate. Nvidia described this as \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress; the specific revenue-share percentage was not disclosed and Nvidia declined to provide further detail.\\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] . … Under the AI Compute Partnership, Nvidia guarantees a floor utilization rate on deployed GPUs. If a neocloud partner's customer\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e79 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider, is one of the first two adopters of Nvidia's AI Compute Partnership and is deploying up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement with Nvidia. Sharon AI listed on Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO and raised an additional $1.6 billion in a June 2026 private placement to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory; it has partnered with VAST Data for 600PB of storage supporting roughly 100,000 GPUs across its sovereign-cloud operations in Australia and Asia-Pacific.\\n    Quote: The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] . … Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e80 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies, a private company, is one of the first two adopters of Nvidia's AI Compute Partnership and is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture that is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] . … Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e100 [third_party_view; barchart.com (article author); original source Seaport Research ; from gnews:ff7d99387d1dae92ede72145 2026-07-05] Analysts at Seaport Research published a note (described as 'last week,' i.e., shortly before the 2026-07-05 article) raising concerns about a 'circular-financing boom' at Nvidia — that Nvidia increasingly finances the companies that buy its chips, either directly via equity or debt or by enabling financing of the increasingly expensive chips — and flagging growing issues on Nvidia's balance sheet. The concern, attributed to Seaport, is that such circular financing could mean the demand investors are seeing is not as real as Nvidia's earnings releases suggest, an issue bears say could hit earnings quality down the line.\\n    Quote: Analysts at Seaport Research put forward a piece last week that was honestly pretty difficult to read, particularly for bulls. That's because many of the issues these analysts noted align with the idea that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues, and there could be plenty of turbulence underneath the surface for investors. … concerns around a \\\"circular-financing boom\\\" put forward by Seaport ana\\n- e109 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\\n    Quote: This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level. … Nvidia’s backstop program is typically \\n- e110 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\\n    Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e111 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\\n    Quote: Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e112 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e542; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e146 [third_party_view; Futurum Group; original source Firmus ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Futurum Group states that Firmus has $25–30 billion in committed off-take, and questions whether the DSX-aligned factories will reach the assumed tokens-per-megawatt efficiency and utilization and whether that off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n    Quote: Whether the DSX-aligned factories reach the tokens-per-megawatt efficiency and utilization the model assumes, and whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n- e148 [third_party_view; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Futurum Group notes that NVIDIA has not disclosed the revenue-share percentage under its new revenue-sharing model or how usage of the supported capacity is measured.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has not disclosed the revenue share percentage or how usage is measured.\\n- e247 [party_statement; Futuriom; NVIDIA blog by Colette Kress and Raj Mirpuri; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA announced (July 1) a new revenue-sharing and credit-support model under which it will guarantee/backstop the sale of GPU capacity for selected AI cloud providers (neoclouds) over several years, earning standard product revenue plus a share of cloud revenue above the guaranteed level. NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP Raj Mirpuri described it as aligning economics with AI clouds through a 'revenue-sharing and credit-support model.' No guarantee amount was disclosed.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares tumbled on recent news that the vendor will guarantee the sale of GPU capacity for selected AI cloud providers (aka neoclouds) over several years. In return, NVIDIA will share in revenues earned above the guaranteed, or backstop, level. … This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a rev\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e248 [party_statement; Futuriom; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA stated that two AI cloud providers have already signed on under the new model: Sharon AI (Australia) will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus (Indonesia) is building a DSX AI factory expected to scale to 360 megawatts and hold up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA states that two AI cloud providers have already signed on for the new deal: Sharon AI, based in Australia, will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan. … Firmus, based in Indoesia, is building a DSX AI factory there that’s expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e249 [rumour; Futuriom; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA reportedly has had a backstop deal with CoreWeave since 2023, agreeing to pay up to $6.3 billion through 2032 if CoreWeave fails to sell a specified amount of NVIDIA GPU capacity — cited as a precedent for the new model.\\n    Quote: There is a precedent to the new NVIDIA model. NVIDIA reportedly has had a backstop deal with CoreWeave since 2023, in which it has agreed to pay up to $6.3 billion through 2032 if the cloud provider fails to sell a specified amount of NVIDIA GPU capacity.\\n- e288 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA (Bank of America) ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA estimates Nvidia's total strategic/ecosystem investments across OpenAI, Anthropic, Intel, CoreWeave, Nebius, Lumentum, Coherent, Marvell and others at around $65 billion — less than 35% of its CY2026E free cash flow and about 17% of its CY2027E free cash flow — responding to the question of whether Nvidia is 'financing its own demand' and concluding the scale looks manageable. This is BofA's estimate, not a company disclosure.\\n    Quote: Strategic investments across OpenAI, Anthropic, Intel, CoreWeave, Nebius, Lumentum, Coherent, Marvell and others also raise a fair question: is Nvidia financing its own demand? BofA’s answer is that the scale still looks manageable. It estimates total ecosystem investments at around $65 billion, less than 35% of CY2026E free cash flow and about 17% of CY2027E free cash flow.\\n- e303 [third_party_view; Vivek Arya, Bank of America; original source Vivek Arya, Bank of America ; from gnews:86c243945145ed901feb26cf 2026-07-09] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya wrote in a note this week that unproductive use of cash in vendor financing, rather than stronger buybacks/dividends, is one of the concerns fueling Nvidia's sell-off.\\n    Quote: Several concerns are fueling the sell-off, Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya wrote in a note this week. They include gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks/dividends.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v4 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's government or a regulator permit NVIDIA to sell H200 AI chips to Chinese customers by 2027-01-31, as shown by an official Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments or revenue to Chinese customers?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; as a market/company reference for this question, that corresponds to rung 3, Still open, rather than a qualifying Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed sale by the deadline. The forecast differs because a \\n- v5 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Commerce Department or Bureau of Industry and Security publicly issue a finding, impose a penalty, or announce a new export-control measure stemming from its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus, U.S. guidance, or stated Commerce/BIS decision deadline is supplied, so the reference remains an unresolved outcome by the due date. The evidence makes a U.S. action more plausible: the investigation is reported, and Singapore charges and broader chip-sm\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much did the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures during calendar 2026, according to their full-year 2026 capex disclosures?: now rung 3 from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). Meta's higher 2026 capex plan, Google's up-to-$190 billion capex guidance, and the report that the big four are planning higher capex all lean upward, but none quantifies th\\n- v8 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?: now rung 3 from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecas\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is no stated market, company, or consensus amount, which sits at rung 3 ($1B to below $5B). The implied total is about $15.25B: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88B backstop, plus an estimated $10.37B for Firmus, scaling the Sharon amount by GPU count to half of Firmus's 170,000-GPU target (85,000 GPUs) because the full target volume is not shown in hand. Firmus's $25–30B of expected customer revenue supports the project's scale but is not itself credit support. The estimate exceeds rung 5's $10B boundary. The CoreWeave backstop is a precedent outside the new model and is not added. The recent evidence does not change the key estimate or its supporting calculation.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is no stated market, company, or consensus amount, which sits in rung 3 ($1B to below $5B). The implied total remains about $15.25B: Sharon AI’s disclosed $4.88B backstop plus an estimated $10.37B for Firmus, scaling Sharon’s amount by GPU count to half of Firmus’s 170,000-GPU target because the evidence does not establish that the full target volume is in hand. Firmus’s reported $25–30B of committed off-take supports the project’s demand, but it is not itself NVIDIA credit support and does not establish a full-volume guarantee amount. The estimate remains above rung 5’s $10B boundary. The reported CoreWeave backstop is a precedent outside the new model and is not included.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e1\",\n    \"e2\",\n    \"e24\",\n    \"e110\",\n    \"e111\",\n    \"e112\",\n    \"e146\",\n    \"e248\",\n    \"e249\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e2\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e13\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e16\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e17\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e24\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e25\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e30\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e31\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e36\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e37\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e38\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e78\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e79\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e80\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e100\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e109\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e110\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e111\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e112\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e146\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e148\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e247\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e248\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e249\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e288\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e303\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and estimated total are unchanged, but new evidence reports Firmus’s $25–30B of expected customer revenue and characterizes it as committed off-take (e112, e146). This supports Firmus demand while not specifying NVIDIA’s backstop amount or proving the full 170,000-GPU target is in hand, so it does not change the discounted sizing estimate.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v8\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"No market, company, or consensus amount was stated when the question opened; the reference is rung 3 ($1B to below $5B).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About $15.25B by 2027-12-31: Sharon AI’s disclosed $4.88B backstop plus an estimated $10.37B for Firmus, using half of its 170,000-GPU target (85,000 GPUs) at Sharon’s implied backstop value per GPU. Firmus’s $25–30B of committed off-take supports the project’s demand but does not state the credit-support amount.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 2,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v5 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"Calendar 2027 data-center capital expenditures by the four largest AI hyperscalers\\\", 2027, due 2028-03-31): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $800 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion but below $900 billion. / rung 3 Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection: Combined spending is at least $900 billion but below $1.1 trillion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion but below $1.2 trillion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $1.2 trillion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 8 items]\\n- e41 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the four largest AI hyperscalers' data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e80 [party_statement; fool.com analyst; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would exceed $1 trillion in 2027, after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41, e149; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e149 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] NVIDIA says the four largest AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80, e390; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e152 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author estimates that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40, e315; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e280 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com 2026-07-10; from gnews:4c201dc59ba0c7579bfef4db 2026-07-10] GuruFocus reports analysts expect NVIDIA’s share within Meta to decline while major cloud providers’ overall AI capital expenditures rise significantly.\\n    Quote: However, analysts remain optimistic about NVIDIA's future, suggesting that while its share within Meta may decline, overall AI capital expenditures from major cloud service providers are expected to rise significantly.\\n- e315 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] NVIDIA told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to exceed $1 trillion next year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e152; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e316 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-12; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] The author estimates that NVIDIA likely already has orders on the books for next year, giving it visibility into demand.\\n    Quote: I'm more inclined to believe Nvidia over the market's skepticism, as it likely has orders on the books for next year, so it can easily see the demand already.\\n- e390 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, reporting Morgan Stanley’s forecast; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in calendar 2027 and add 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity that year.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e149; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. NVIDIA’s stated forecast of more than $1 trillion, alongside the $650 billion 2026 projection, lifts the implied outcome modestly above the reference; the order-book view adds support for demand, and the broader AI-capex view is directionally supportive. But the evidence does not quantify spending at $1.1 trillion or more, so the most likely outcome remains in rung 3.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e41\",\n    \"e80\",\n    \"e149\",\n    \"e152\",\n    \"e280\",\n    \"e315\",\n    \"e316\",\n    \"e390\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e41\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e80\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e149\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e152\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e280\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e315\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e316\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e390\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and implied outcome are unchanged: the evidence still points to spending above roughly $1 trillion, without establishing a figure of $1.1 trillion or more. The repeated NVIDIA claim and Morgan Stanley’s similarly sized forecast add no new quantified basis to move the rung.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection for calendar 2027 is the reference and sits in rung 3 ($900 billion to below $1.1 trillion).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"NVIDIA’s forecast is above $1 trillion, following a projected $650 billion in 2026—an increase of more than about 54%. The evidence supports spending around or modestly above $1 trillion, but does not quantify a level at or above $1.1 trillion.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 3,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v11 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \\\"China authorization for named AI firms to purchase NVIDIA H200 chips\\\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will China authorize Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: China has authorized all three named firms to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31. / rung 2 No: China has not authorized all three named firms to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 19 items]\\n- e231 [rumour; technologyreview.com; original source Information ; from gnews:50a84fa6b2f3eb33f29948a4 2026-07-09] China reportedly plans to permit Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips; the report gives no date for the planned permission.\\n    Quote: China plans to let its top AI firms buy Nvidia H200 chips\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e233; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e232 [fact; South China Morning Post; original source U.S. government ; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] The U.S. government approved exports of NVIDIA’s H200 GPU to China in early 2026; the report says Beijing had restricted Chinese firms from purchasing the chips.\\n    Quote: In early 2026, Washington took the rare step of approving Nvidia’s H200 graphics processing unit for export to China, but Beijing has deliberately restricted Chinese firms from purchasing them as it pursues a tech self-sufficiency drive.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e395, e423; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e233 [rumour; An unnamed source with knowledge of the matter, quoted by the South China Morning Post; original source South China Morning Post 2026-07-09; from gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09] China’s government reportedly plans to let selected companies, including Alibaba Group Holding, buy limited numbers of NVIDIA H200 GPUs; the report gives no date or quantity.\\n    Quote: Now, however, China’s stance is beginning to change, as the government plans to let selected companies – including Alibaba Group Holding – buy limited numbers of the device, a source with knowledge of the matter told the South China Morning Post.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e231; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e268 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA’s Q2 FY2027 guidance excluded Data Center compute revenue from China, and NVIDIA reported zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance explicitly excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, and there were zero H20 shipments in the quarter.\\n- e284 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St., recounting NVIDIA’s guidance assumption; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA’s Q2 FY2027 revenue outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: Notably, that outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China, meaning any policy thaw on H200 exports could represent additional upside.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e226, e371, e413; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e363 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-13; from gnews:f4eb5206f2995f5dbb86c5e9 2026-07-13] The author reports a $2.5 billion black-market chip-smuggling scheme involving Nvidia chips.\\n    Quote: there has been a $2.5 billion chip-smuggling scheme on the black market\\n- e394 [party_statement; Jeffery Kessler, quoted by CNBC; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security Jeffery Kessler said very few H200 chips and equivalents had shipped to China and Hong Kong under licenses.\\n    Quote: The bottom line is very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It's a very small quantity of chips,\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e402, e442; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e395 [fact; CNBC; original source U.S. government ; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] The U.S. government issued licenses for NVIDIA H200 chip sales to China earlier in 2026.\\n    Quote: Licenses for the chips, which some in the administration say can be used for military purposes, were issued earlier this year .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e232; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e396 [party_statement; Jeffery Kessler, quoted by CNBC; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Jeffery Kessler said U.S. H200 export-license applications are assessed case by case, with national-security requirements and compliance inspections.\\n    Quote: Kessler said that the U.S. government assessed companies that want the H200 chips on a case-by-case basis, with applicants needing to meet national security requirements and submit to inspections to make sure the chips are compliant.\\n- e397 [party_statement; Jeffery Kessler, quoted by CNBC; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Jeffery Kessler said the U.S. government denies some applications for H200 export licenses.\\n    Quote: There are cases where we deny the license applications we receive,\\n- e402 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source U.S. commerce official ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] A U.S. commerce official told Congress that NVIDIA H200 chip shipments to China had begun, with very few shipped so far.\\n    Quote: A top U.S. commerce official told Congress that while the number of H200 chips shipped to China so far was \\\"very few,\\\" the shipments have commenced.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e394; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e413 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] NVIDIA's guidance excludes Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: Guidance also excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e284; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e421 [rumour; Tom's Hardware, reporting the Financial Times; original source Unnamed sources cited by the Financial Times ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] Unnamed sources cited by the Financial Times said NVIDIA cut its roster of authorized Asian customers by more than half and used tougher inspections, including customer-site visits, contract checks, and end-user interviews.\\n    Quote: According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer d\\n- e422 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-07-14; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] Tom's Hardware said the US and allied crackdown on AI GPU smuggling had reduced AI GPU supply in China, making it harder for Chinese AI companies to obtain processors.\\n    Quote: Washington’s and its allies’ crackdown on AI GPU smuggling have cut supply in China, which is now making it harder for AI companies to procure the processors they need.\\n- e423 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Beijing ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] The article said Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA H200 GPUs that the US had permitted NVIDIA to export to select regional customers.\\n    Quote: However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e232; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e424 [rumour; Tom's Hardware, reporting the Financial Times; original source An unnamed tech executive cited by the Financial Times ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that Chinese domestic semiconductor suppliers were sold out and companies were considering less powerful chips instead.\\n    Quote: One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\\n- e437 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed sources familiar with the matter ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] Unnamed sources said some Chinese cloud firms recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain NVIDIA H200 chips, indicating progress in Chinese import reviews; no specific firms or timing were given.\\n    Quote: However, some Chinese cloud firms have recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain H200 chips, the sources said, indicating some progress in import reviews by Chinese authorities.\\n- e441 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware, citing Reuters; original source Reuters ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported that Chinese technology firms had more than two million NVIDIA H200 chips on order about six months before 2026-07-14, exceeding NVIDIA's available inventory at that time.\\n    Quote: But even with those domestic production initiatives, the Chinese hunger for AI silicon is so deep that six months ago, Reuters said the nation's tech firms had more than two million H200 chips on order, far more than what Nvidia had on hand at the time.\\n- e442 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware, citing CNBC; original source A U.S. trade official 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official said at a congressional hearing on 2026-07-14 that very few H200 and equivalent chips had shipped against licenses, describing the quantity as very small.\\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \\\"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It’s a very small quantity of chips\\\" during a congressional hearing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e394; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The available reference is Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus is supplied. The report that China plans to permit purchases is a meaningful push toward rung 1, but it is undated and does not establish that all three firms will be authorized by the deadline. NVIDIA’s zero-China-revenue outlook is consistent with continued constraints, though it does not directly determine authorization. On balance, the evidence implies that authorization for all three by 2027-01-31 is less likely than not, so it does not cross the rung boundary.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus is supplied. The specific report that China plans to permit the three named firms, together with Reuters’ report of progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon obtain H200s, makes authorization of all three by the deadline more likely than continued non-authorization. This crosses the binary rung boundary, though the reports do not establish that authorization has already occurred. The reported refusal and the lack of a stated date or confirmed approvals temper, but do not outweigh, the six-month-plus window.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e231\",\n    \"e233\",\n    \"e232\",\n    \"e423\",\n    \"e437\",\n    \"e394\",\n    \"e402\",\n    \"e395\",\n    \"e268\",\n    \"e284\",\n    \"e413\",\n    \"e424\",\n    \"e441\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e231\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e232\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e233\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e268\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e284\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e363\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e394\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e395\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e396\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e397\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e402\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e413\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e421\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e422\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e423\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e424\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e437\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e441\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e442\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung changes from 2 to 1 because the specific report of planned permission for the named firms and the newer report of progress in import reviews make authorization by the due date more likely than not; the reports of very few shipments and Beijing’s earlier refusal remain counterevidence.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s is the reference and sits at rung 2 (No); no market consensus is supplied.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies authorization of Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek by 2027-01-31 (rung 1). This is a forecast, not an established outcome: the reported plan names the firms but gives no date, while the separate report says import reviews are progressing and firms may soon obtain chips.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 4,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v12 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"Aggregate incremental compute capacity added by Google, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft\\\", 2027, due 2028-03-31): How much incremental compute capacity will Google, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft add in calendar 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Below 10 GW: Less than 10 GW / rung 2 10 to under 15 GW: At least 10 GW and less than 15 GW / rung 3 15 to under 20 GW: At least 15 GW and less than 20 GW / rung 4 20 to under 25 GW: At least 20 GW and less than 25 GW / rung 5 25 GW or more: At least 25 GW\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 3 items]\\n- e390 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, reporting Morgan Stanley’s forecast; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in calendar 2027 and add 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity that year.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e149; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e391 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source 247wallst.com 2026-07-13; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] The article reports that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta added roughly 6.7 GW of compute capacity in calendar 2025.\\n    Quote: In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW.\\n- e392 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, reporting Morgan Stanley’s forecast; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google alone will add 6.8 GW of compute capacity in calendar 2027, more than the four hyperscalers added combined in calendar 2025.\\n    Quote: Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"No consensus or company guidance is supplied. The only quantified market expectation shown is Morgan Stanley’s 19.5 GW forecast, which sits in rung 3. That forecast also implies rung 3: 19.5 GW is at least 15 GW but below 20 GW, so it does not cross into rung 4. The 2025 figure is historical context, not a basis for a separate run-rate extrapolation; Google’s 6.8 GW forecast is consistent with, and does not exceed, the four-company total.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e390\",\n    \"e391\",\n    \"e392\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e390\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e391\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e392\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"This is the first judgement.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan is supplied. The only quantified expectation is Morgan Stanley’s 19.5 GW forecast, which is rung 3.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"19.5 GW in calendar 2027, using Morgan Stanley’s explicit four-company forecast; this falls in rung 3. Google’s 6.8 GW is part of that total, leaving 12.7 GW implied for the other three.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 5,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Whether NVIDIA launches the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by the end of 2027.\\\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will NVIDIA launch the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA launches the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31. / rung 2 No: NVIDIA has not launched the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture by 2027-12-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 21 items]\\n- e123 [rumour; Investing.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis claimed that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture would be delayed to 2028 because of PCB midplane manufacturing challenges.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153, e345; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e124 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA spokesperson said the company's product roadmap remained intact, disputing reports of delays to the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture.\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e147, e154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e132 [rumour; businesstimes.com.sg; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 faced printed-circuit-board construction setbacks; the report says the system was delayed by more than a year.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e146, e182; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e133 [third_party_view; Shawn Oh, quoted by businesstimes.com.sg; original source Shawn Oh, head of Korea cash equities at NH Investment & Securities ; from gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0 2026-07-06] NH Investment & Securities’ Shawn Oh said the prospect of a Kyber NVL144 delay was raising uncertainty around NVIDIA’s next-generation scale-out roadmap and widening the competitive window for alternative AI platforms.\\n    Quote: The prospect of a Kyber NVL144 delay together with other points in the post are “raising uncertainty around Nvidia’s next-generation scale-out roadmap and creating a wider competitive window for alternative AI platforms,” he added.\\n- e146 [third_party_view; cnbc.com; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] SemiAnalysis claimed NVIDIA was at least a year behind on manufacturing its next line of server racks, the Kyber generation.\\n    Quote: a research report claiming the AI leader was at least a year behind on manufacturing of its next line of server racks.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e132; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e147 [party_statement; cnbc.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] NVIDIA disputed SemiAnalysis’s report that its next-generation Kyber server rack was running into delays.\\n    Quote: the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e124; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e153 [third_party_view; 24/7 Wall St.; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 launch has slipped from 2027 to 2028.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e123, e223; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e154 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St., reporting NVIDIA’s statement to Bloomberg; original source NVIDIA spokesperson ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] NVIDIA said its roadmap remains intact, disputing the report that Kyber NVL144 was delayed to 2028.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA publicly disputed the report on Monday, with a spokesperson telling Bloomberg its “roadmap is intact.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e124, e183, e224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e155 [third_party_view; 24/7 Wall St., quoting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] SemiAnalysis said the 78-layer PCB midplane remains challenging to manufacture.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis said it “remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.”\\n- e156 [third_party_view; 24/7 Wall St.; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA’s NVL576 may also be delayed or initially limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: A larger sibling, the NVL576, designed to link eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small initial volumes, according to SemiAnalysis.\\n- e182 [third_party_view; Futuriom, reporting SemiAnalysis’s X post; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack system had encountered manufacturing setbacks.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e132; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e183 [party_statement; Futuriom, quoting an NVIDIA spokesperson; original source NVIDIA spokesperson ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] An NVIDIA spokesperson said the company’s product roadmap remains intact.\\n    Quote: “Our road map is intact.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154, e346; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e223 [rumour; barchart.com; original source Unnamed reports cited by barchart.com ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Reports suggested NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028.\\n    Quote: Recently, Nvidia faced reports that suggested its next-generation Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e224 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] NVIDIA denied speculation that Kyber would be delayed and said its roadmap remains on track.\\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154, e312; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e234 [party_statement; IEEE Spectrum; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a4eb2bdfabca94f5887881c6 2026-07-09] NVIDIA plans to scale its interconnected systems from 72 GPUs to as many as 576 by 2027.\\n    Quote: At the same time, Nvidia is planning to add even more processors to single interconnected systems, going from 72 GPUs today to as many as 576 by 2027.\\n- e311 [rumour; Jim Cramer, quoted by finance.yahoo.com; original source Semi Analysis ; from gnews:1c3c0c58ed5ec3da43292f9b 2026-07-11] Semi Analysis reportedly said that delays to one part could make all of NVIDIA’s products late; no timing or scale was specified.\\n    Quote: You know Semi Analysis does pretty good work, saying that everything could be late for NVIDIA because one particular part of their business is late.\\n- e312 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1c3c0c58ed5ec3da43292f9b 2026-07-11] NVIDIA denied reports that the Kyber platform is being delayed.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) has denied reports that the Kyber platform is being delayed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e345 [third_party_view; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system could be delayed until 2028 because of manufacturing problems with a crucial circuit board.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e123, e443; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e346 [party_statement; fool.com (Geoffrey Seiler); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said its product roadmap remains intact despite the reported possible Kyber NVL144 delay.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has come out and said its roadmap remains intact, although questions linger.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e183, e444; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e443 [rumour; ebc.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] SemiAnalysis reportedly suggested over the weekend that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 architecture could be delayed into 2028.\\n    Quote: industry research firm SemiAnalysis suggesting that Nvidia’s upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e345; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e444 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, quoted by ebc.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] An NVIDIA spokesperson said the company’s roadmap was intact, responding to reports of possible Kyber NVL144 delays.\\n    Quote: the company's \\\"roadmap is intact,\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e346; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v11 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus is supplied. The specific report that China plans to permit the three named firms, together with Reuters’ report of progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon o\\n- v12 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much incremental compute capacity will Google, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft add in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 15 to under 20 GW; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: No consensus or company guidance is supplied. The only quantified market expectation shown is Morgan Stanley’s 19.5 GW forecast, which sits in rung 3. That forecast also implies rung 3: 19.5 GW is at least 15 GW but below 20 GW, so it does not cross into rung 4. The 2025 figure is historical context\\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around the reported $650 billion plan; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence does not establish a quantified change to the four companies’ 2026 data-center spending: Meta’s $125–145 billion and Google’s $190 billion figures are total-capex or infrastructure budgets, not the data\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The specific SemiAnalysis report says Kyber NVL144 has slipped from 2027 to 2028 because of PCB manufacturing challenges, implying a launch after the deadline and crossing to rung 2. NVIDIA’s “Our roadmap is intact” is a counterweight, but it gives no specific launch date or operational detail; the repeated coverage is one report, not independent confirmation. The broad plan to scale interconnected systems to as many as 576 GPUs by 2027 is not specific confirmation of a Kyber NVL144 launch.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The specific SemiAnalysis report says Kyber NVL144 has slipped from its planned 2027 launch to 2028, citing PCB manufacturing challenges; that makes a launch after the 2027-12-31 deadline the more likely outcome and crosses the binary rung boundary. NVIDIA’s “Our roadmap is intact” is a counterweight, but it gives no specific launch timing or operational detail. The broader plan to scale interconnected systems to as many as 576 GPUs by 2027 is not specific confirmation of a Kyber NVL144 launch.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e123\",\n    \"e124\",\n    \"e153\",\n    \"e155\",\n    \"e234\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e123\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e124\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e132\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e133\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e146\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e147\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e153\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e154\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e155\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e156\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e182\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e183\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e223\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e224\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e234\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e311\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e312\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e345\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e346\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e443\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e444\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and implied 2028 timing remain the same. The recent reports repeat the same SemiAnalysis delay claim and NVIDIA’s broad rebuttal; the manufacturing detail is consistent with the PCB challenge already supporting the previous conclusion.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap is the reference; it sits at rung 1 (Yes).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely outcome is that Kyber NVL144 launches in 2028, after the 2027-12-31 deadline. This follows from the specific report that its planned 2027 launch slipped to 2028 because of PCB manufacturing challenges, discounted against NVIDIA’s nonspecific denial.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 6,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v4 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"Calendar 2026 data-center capital expenditures by the four largest AI hyperscalers\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\\nLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion but below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral comparison: around the reported $650 billion plan: Combined spending is at least $600 billion but below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion but below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 11 items]\\n- e40 [fact; fool.com; original source The four largest AI hyperscalers ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The four largest AI hyperscalers plan around $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e148, e152; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e80 [party_statement; fool.com analyst; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would exceed $1 trillion in 2027, after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41, e149; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e111 [third_party_view; Allen Harris; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimated that annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026\\n- e122 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com; original source Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments said the reported Kyber delay did not necessarily mean overall AI spending would shrink.\\n    Quote: A Kyber delay doesn't necessarily mean overall AI spending will shrink, said Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments, according to Bloomberg .\\n- e148 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The author estimates that the four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e152 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author estimates that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40, e315; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e161 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Google ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google guided 2026 capital expenditures to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\\n- e280 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com 2026-07-10; from gnews:4c201dc59ba0c7579bfef4db 2026-07-10] GuruFocus reports analysts expect NVIDIA’s share within Meta to decline while major cloud providers’ overall AI capital expenditures rise significantly.\\n    Quote: However, analysts remain optimistic about NVIDIA's future, suggesting that while its share within Meta may decline, overall AI capital expenditures from major cloud service providers are expected to rise significantly.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e386 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Meta ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta raised its 2026 capital-expenditure forecast to $125–145 billion and launched Meta Compute to rent excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion, then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\\n- e398 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source Meta, Microsoft, Alphabet, and Amazon ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Meta, Microsoft, Alphabet, and Amazon are planning higher capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: Hyperscalers, including Meta Platforms , Microsoft , Alphabet , and Amazon , are planning higher capital expenditures in 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e280; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e412 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Meta ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] Meta has a $145 billion infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon, which could bear on demand for NVIDIA chips.\\n    Quote: Meta’s $145B infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which is in rung 3. The evidence still implies spending around that level: Meta's raised $125–145 billion 2026 capex forecast adds upward pressure, but it is total capex and the evidence gives neither its prior forecast nor the data-center portion, so it does not support a quantified addition to the four-company data-center total. Google's $190 billion guidance is also total capex. Goldman Sachs's $765 billion estimate covers broader AI-infrastructure capex, not this four-hyperscaler data-center measure. The implied outcome therefore remains below the $700 billion rung-4 boundary and above $600 billion.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence does not establish a quantified change to the four companies’ 2026 data-center spending: Meta’s $125–145 billion and Google’s $190 billion figures are total-capex or infrastructure budgets, not the data-center portion of the four-company total. The broader $765 billion AI-infrastructure estimate is not the same scope, and the general statements about rising spending do not quantify an adjustment. The implied total therefore remains below rung 4’s $700 billion boundary and above rung 2’s $600 billion boundary.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e40\",\n    \"e80\",\n    \"e111\",\n    \"e122\",\n    \"e148\",\n    \"e152\",\n    \"e161\",\n    \"e280\",\n    \"e386\",\n    \"e398\",\n    \"e412\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e40\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e80\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e111\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e122\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e148\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e152\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e161\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e280\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e386\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e398\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e412\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The conclusion remains at rung 3 and the evidence supplies no new quantified, scope-matched adjustment to the implied 2026 total. Meta’s raised total-capex forecast was already part of the previous rationale; the other broad or repeated claims do not change the key figure.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"The reported plan is around $650 billion for calendar 2026, placing the market/reference expectation in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Around $650 billion: retain the reported four-hyperscaler data-center plan because the newer company budgets are not broken out into the relevant data-center portion, while the $765 billion estimate covers broader AI-infrastructure spending.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 7,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v8 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"The sum of calendar-2027 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, compared with the over-$1-trillion figure NVIDIA management forecast for 2027.\\\", 2027, due 2028-03-30): What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?\\nLadder: rung 1 below 800,000,000,000 USD: below 800,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD: from 800,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD: from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027\\\") / rung 4 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD: from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,500,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more: 1,500,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 17 items]\\n- e125 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] On its most recent quarterly conference call, NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would top $1 trillion in 2027, after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e83; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e55 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom's Hardware reported that public opposition to data centers — blamed for large increases in power/utility costs and water consumption, with 7 in 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026, a demand signal for AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: This has become a major national issue recently, with data centers being blamed for massive increases in power and utility costs, as well as increased water consumption and a reduction in the quality of life in the communities that surround these developments. … These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e114 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\\n    Quote: Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\\n- e147 [party_statement; Futurum Group; original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, and that 100 gigawatts are expected to be online before the end of the decade.\\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\\n- e161 [third_party_view; autonocion.com, citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] More than 75 data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom's Hardware, and polling cited in the same reporting found 7 in 10 Americans oppose a data center built near their home.\\n    Quote: With over $130 billion in data center projects already blocked or delayed by communities fed up with power and water demands, a zero-local-water nuclear option isn't just an engineering story — it's a potential unlock for the entire AI buildout. … More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e55; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e197 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The article reports that Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027).\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e83; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e205 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source AI hyperscalers (capex plans; 2027 forecast is the author's) ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e83; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e250 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by Futuriom); original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported (July 1) that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models, on the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model; the article notes Meta had planned to buy 'millions' of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year and questions what its move implies for AI demand.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\\n- e256 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings, a data-center electrical and technology systems contractor, reported March-quarter 2026 results: Communications segment revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which it attributed to continued strong demand in the data center market; Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million; and total backlog rose 62% since the end of fiscal 2025 to $3.86 billion, driven by customer demand and expansion capacity.\\n    Quote: Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” adding that “the recent capital investments we have made have positioned us well to respond to that demand and deliver solutions to our customers.” … Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million, and total backlog exploded to $3.86 \\n- e257 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services, a grid and electrical infrastructure contractor, reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus, and backlog that rose to a record $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $34.70–35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55–14.25 in adjusted EPS, and cited a path to more than double adjusted EPS by 2030.\\n    Quote: Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion. Management raised full-year 2026 guidance to a range of $34.70 billion to $35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55 to $14.25 in adjusted EPS.\\n- e258 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA, a mechanical/HVAC contractor, reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year (51% organic), diluted EPS of $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus, and backlog of $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion a year earlier; data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a year earlier. … Data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue\\n- e259 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA (CFO Bill George) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] On the Q1 2026 call, Comfort Systems USA finance chief Bill George said the constraint on the business is now a supply (labor) issue rather than demand, saying 'There is plenty more work we could take if we could possibly do it' — indicating data-center work demand exceeds the contractor's capacity to execute.\\n    Quote: Today, I think it’s really important for people to understand that it’s a supply issue. There is plenty more work we could take if we could possibly do it.\\n- e260 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 diluted EPS of $6.84 versus $5.90 consensus, Remaining Performance Obligations at a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50–19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25–29.75.\\n    Quote: Diluted EPS of $6.84 beat the $5.90 consensus by 15.85%, Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50 billion to $19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25 to $29.75.\\n- e261 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 (beating consensus by a double-digit percentage), revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin; it raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 crushed the consensus by a double-digit percentage, revenue jumped 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue rocketed 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin. Management raised the full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40 to $19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n- e395 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year (2027).\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\\n- e404 [party_statement; tech-insider.org; original source Nvidia (reporting AWS's plan) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\\n- e469 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey (247wallst.com); original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley, cited in a 247wallst.com article, forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure 'next year' (calendar 2027), adding about 19.5 gigawatts of incremental compute capacity, up from roughly 6.7 GW added in 2025; Google alone is expected to add 6.8 GW.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast that the four major hyperscalers’ 2027 spending will top $1 trillion, which sits at rung 3. The evidence continues to imply spending above $1 trillion, but does not support a sufficiently specific 2027, four-company data-center-capex figure to cross the $1.2 trillion boundary: contractor demand and AWS’s GPU deployment plan support the buildout, while reported project delays and the Meta demand question are counterweights. Broader AI-capex projections and multi-year or all-industry infrastructure ambitions do not translate directly into this question’s 2027 total.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecasts over $1 trillion, while its 19.5 GW capacity estimate does not translate into a specific capex total. Project delays and execution constraints are counterweights, but are not quantified as reductions to the four hyperscalers’ 2027 spend. Broader AI-capex forecasts and supplier activity do not establish a higher four-company, 2027 data-center-capex figure.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e125\",\n    \"e55\",\n    \"e114\",\n    \"e147\",\n    \"e161\",\n    \"e197\",\n    \"e205\",\n    \"e250\",\n    \"e256\",\n    \"e257\",\n    \"e258\",\n    \"e259\",\n    \"e260\",\n    \"e261\",\n    \"e395\",\n    \"e404\",\n    \"e469\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e125\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e55\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e114\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e147\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e161\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e197\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e205\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e250\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e256\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e257\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e258\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e259\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e260\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e261\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e395\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e404\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e469\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung is unchanged, but the supporting evidence now includes Morgan Stanley’s separate over-$1-trillion forecast and 19.5 GW capacity estimate, plus a reported roughly $130 billion of delayed or blocked projects. These update the factual basis and counterweights without establishing a figure above $1.2 trillion.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA management’s forecast that the four major hyperscalers’ 2027 spending will top $1 trillion. That expectation sits at rung 3, which begins at $1 trillion; the forecast does not specify a level reaching rung 4.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"More than $1 trillion, but not supported at $1.2 trillion or more, for calendar 2027. NVIDIA’s forecast follows a projected $650 billion in 2026 and Morgan Stanley independently forecasts over $1 trillion for 2027; neither supplies a precise total above the rung-4 boundary.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 8,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"A final decision by the French competition authority on its antitrust probe into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU practices.\\\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will the French competition authority publish a final decision on its antitrust investigation into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU markets by 2027-01-31?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: The French competition authority publishes a final decision on its antitrust investigation into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU markets by 2027-01-31. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-07-09, the French competition authority formally abandons or withdraws the investigation without publishing a final decision. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-09 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 1 items]\\n- e341 [fact; moomoo; original source French competition authority ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] The French competition authority announced Thursday that its antitrust investigation into Nvidia is nearing a conclusion; the probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\\n    Quote: Regulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v4 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's government or a regulator permit NVIDIA to sell H200 AI chips to Chinese customers by 2027-01-31, as shown by an official Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments or revenue to Chinese customers?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; as a market/company reference for this question, that corresponds to rung 3, Still open, rather than a qualifying Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed sale by the deadline. The forecast differs because a \\n- v5 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Commerce Department or Bureau of Industry and Security publicly issue a finding, impose a penalty, or announce a new export-control measure stemming from its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus, U.S. guidance, or stated Commerce/BIS decision deadline is supplied, so the reference remains an unresolved outcome by the due date. The evidence makes a U.S. action more plausible: the investigation is reported, and Singapore charges and broader chip-sm\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much did the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures during calendar 2026, according to their full-year 2026 capex disclosures?: now rung 3 from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). Meta's higher 2026 capex plan, Google's up-to-$190 billion capex guidance, and the report that the big four are planning higher capex all lean upward, but none quantifies th\\n- v8 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?: now rung 3 from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecas\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is rung 3, Still open: no consensus or formal decision deadline is supplied, and no final decision is reported. The authority’s statement that the probe is nearing a conclusion makes publication by the deadline the more likely forecast; there is no evidence of formal abandonment to support rung 2.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is rung 3, Still open: no consensus or formal decision deadline is supplied. The authority’s statement that the investigation is nearing a conclusion makes publication by 2027-01-31 the more likely outcome, rather than a formal abandonment; it supports rung 1 as a forecast, though it does not establish that a decision has already been published.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e341\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e341\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and key supporting fact are unchanged from the previous conclusion: the authority said the probe was nearing a conclusion. No new evidence or relevant theme changes the forecast.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated decision deadline is supplied; the reference is rung 3, Still open.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"A final decision is more likely than not to be published by 2027-01-31. The authority’s 2026-07-09 statement that the investigation was nearing a conclusion supports completion within the roughly six months remaining, but supplies no exact publication date.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 9,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply commitments (purchase and capacity commitments) disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion cited in this item.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What dollar amount of NVIDIA’s supply, purchase and capacity commitments will be disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 90,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 90,000,000,000 USD to below 110,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 110,000,000,000 USD to below 130,000,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \\\"Supply commitments of $119 billion\\\") / rung 4 Bearish: from 130,000,000,000 USD to below 150,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 150,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 13 items]\\n- e154 [fact; Omor Ibne Ehsan, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA carries $119 billion of supply commitments, which the article says compounds demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow.\\n    Quote: Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e274, e357, e372, e465, e495, e509, e531; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e133 [fact; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA carries $119 billion in supply commitments, which the article says hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, a question it says the market is already pricing in.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\\n- e186 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Business Times); original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey report) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] The Bloomberg Intelligence report said a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms such as Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corporation, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit, as bottlenecks shift from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips.\\n    Quote: But a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corporation, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to reap the benefits. … The bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e273; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e207 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Micron Technology announced that tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028, according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e403; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e208 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Fool.com (Keithen Drury) ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Fool.com's Keithen Drury forecasts that new capacity in the memory industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half from mid-2026, which could lead to even higher memory chip prices because demand is not slowing.\\n    Quote: As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e378; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e274 [third_party_view; Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya, via Investing.com article published by Yahoo Finance UK; original source Bank of America (analyst Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] In a note on Nvidia, Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said investors \\\"overstate HBM cost pressure while underestimating NVDA's pricing power, scale, and $119 billion of supply-chain commitments,\\\" citing $119 billion of NVIDIA supply-chain commitments as part of his rebuttal to the margin-pressure concern.\\n    Quote: On margins, Arya said investors \\\"overstate HBM cost pressure while underestimating NVDA's pricing power, scale, and $119 billion of supply-chain commitments.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e357 [fact; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA cited $119 billion in total supply commitments, with OpenAI, Anthropic and Meta named as multi-gigawatt customers.\\n    Quote: backed by $119 billion in total supply commitments and OpenAI, Anthropic, and Meta all named as multi-gigawatt customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e372 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (Thomas Richmond); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion in its Q1 FY2027 results, per 24/7 Wall St.'s July 10, 2026 article.\\n    Quote: On the demand side, NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e400 [fact; tech-insider.org; original source Samsung, SK Hynix and Micron (via CNBC reporting) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Samsung, SK Hynix and Micron have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts to AI data-center customers, as hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta and Amazon buy high-bandwidth memory as fast as it can be produced, per CNBC reporting from January 2026.\\n    Quote: Hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon are buying up high-bandwidth memory as fast as Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron can produce it, and all three manufacturers have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, according to CNBC’s reporting from January 2026.\\n- e465 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] NVIDIA carries $119 billion in supply commitments (described as supply obligations) tied to hyperscaler order books, according to the article.\\n    Quote: while NVIDIA carries $119B in supply obligations tied to hyperscalers. … NVIDIA, by contrast, carries $119 billion in supply commitments, tying its fortunes to hyperscaler order books .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e495 [fact; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] NVIDIA had $119 billion in supply commitments, cited alongside $96.58 billion of FY26 free cash flow.\\n    Quote: plausible given $96.58 billion FY26 free cash flow and $119 billion in supply commitments\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e509 [fact; Vandita Jadeja, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA had $119 billion in supply commitments, as the article notes.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is spending like a company that already won , with $119 billion in supply commitments, an $80 billion buyback authorization, and a dividend hike from $0.01 to $0.25 per share.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e531 [fact; EBC (ebc.com); original source NVIDIA Corp. ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Nvidia has committed $119 billion to secure its supply chain, according to EBC's report on the stock's pullback; the article says soaring high-bandwidth memory (HBM) costs, with suppliers SK Hynix and Micron named, are raising alarms about Nvidia's long-term gross margins.\\n    Quote: Supply Chain Commitments: Nvidia has committed a staggering $119 billion to secure its supply chain, but the soaring costs of HBM are raising alarms about the company's long-term gross margins. … Although Nvidia is expected to command incredibly high margins on its upcoming next-generation Rubin architecture, the short-term financial pressure from suppliers like SK Hynix and Micron remains a thorn\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v4 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's government or a regulator permit NVIDIA to sell H200 AI chips to Chinese customers by 2027-01-31, as shown by an official Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments or revenue to Chinese customers?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; as a market/company reference for this question, that corresponds to rung 3, Still open, rather than a qualifying Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed sale by the deadline. The forecast differs because a \\n- v5 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Commerce Department or Bureau of Industry and Security publicly issue a finding, impose a penalty, or announce a new export-control measure stemming from its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus, U.S. guidance, or stated Commerce/BIS decision deadline is supplied, so the reference remains an unresolved outcome by the due date. The evidence makes a U.S. action more plausible: the investigation is reported, and Singapore charges and broader chip-sm\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much did the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures during calendar 2026, according to their full-year 2026 capex disclosures?: now rung 3 from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). Meta's higher 2026 capex plan, Google's up-to-$190 billion capex guidance, and the report that the big four are planning higher capex all lean upward, but none quantifies th\\n- v8 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?: now rung 3 from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecas\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is the last reported $119 billion of supply-related commitments, which is in rung 3 ($110 billion to below $130 billion). The reported amount is a stock, not a quarterly flow to annualize. Persistent memory constraints, long-term contracted HBM output and named multi-gigawatt customers tilt the forecast upward, but none quantifies an increase in NVIDIA's commitments. The implied amount remains around $119 billion, with an upward bias, and does not cross the $130 billion boundary.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The $119 billion reported amount is the reference and sits in rung 3 ($110 billion to below $130 billion). The HBM constraints and higher HBM costs tilt the forecast upward, but no evidence quantifies an increase in NVIDIA's commitments; the evidence therefore does not support crossing the $130 billion boundary.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e372\",\n    \"e207\",\n    \"e208\",\n    \"e400\",\n    \"e531\",\n    \"e357\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e154\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e133\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e186\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e207\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e208\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e274\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e357\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e372\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e400\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e465\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e495\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e509\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e531\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and implied level remain in rung 3, but e531 adds an explicit HBM-cost pressure that could lift the dollar value of commitments, beyond the previously noted supply constraints. It is unquantified and does not establish a move across $130 billion.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v4\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v8\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"The last reported $119 billion of supply-related commitments is the reference; it sits in rung 3 ($110 billion to below $130 billion).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About $119 billion, with an upward bias: the Q1 FY2027 reported commitment stock provides the current level, while the evidence gives no quantified change to carry forward or add. The implied amount remains below the $130 billion rung-4 boundary.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 10,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v8 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA stand-alone CPU sales\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Stand-alone CPU revenue is at least $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Stand-alone CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $25 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Stand-alone CPU revenue is at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Stand-alone CPU revenue is at least $10 billion and less than $15 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Stand-alone CPU revenue is less than $10 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 6 items]\\n- e55 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA plans to launch its first stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\\n- e285 [fact; quantumzeitgeist.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b666d35e5247a8ddcc2d7c72 2026-07-10] NVIDIA unveiled its Vera CPU, designed for agentic AI workloads.\\n    Quote: The newly unveiled NVIDIA Vera CPU is specifically architected to address the demands of agentic AI\\n- e286 [fact; quantumzeitgeist.com; original source Perplexity ; from gnews:b666d35e5247a8ddcc2d7c72 2026-07-10] Perplexity plans to integrate NVIDIA Vera CPUs into its upcoming production systems.\\n    Quote: Perplexity is now planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems\\n- e306 [fact; tikr.com; original source Perplexity ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] Perplexity chose to use NVIDIA’s new CPUs for AI-agent tasks; the item gives no scale or timing.\\n    Quote: AI startup Perplexity has also chosen to use Nvidia’s new CPUs for AI agent tasks.\\n- e332 [third_party_view; barchart.com author; original source Wedbush Securities ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] Wedbush Securities said NVIDIA’s Vera CPU platform could expand its addressable market beyond graphics processors and create billions of dollars in additional revenue; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: Wedbush Securities recently argued that Nvidia's new Vera CPU platform could expand the company's total addressable market (TAM) well beyond graphics processors, opening the door to billions of dollars in additional revenue.\\n- e333 [rumour; barchart.com author; original source Unnamed reports ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] Reports said NVIDIA has begun offering Vera processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow; no customer or date was specified.\\n    Quote: Reports also suggest Nvidia has begun offering the new processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v11 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus is supplied. The specific report that China plans to permit the three named firms, together with Reuters’ report of progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon o\\n- v12 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much incremental compute capacity will Google, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft add in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 15 to under 20 GW; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: No consensus or company guidance is supplied. The only quantified market expectation shown is Morgan Stanley’s 19.5 GW forecast, which sits in rung 3. That forecast also implies rung 3: 19.5 GW is at least 15 GW but below 20 GW, so it does not cross into rung 4. The 2025 figure is historical context\\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around the reported $650 billion plan; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence does not establish a quantified change to the four companies’ 2026 data-center spending: Meta’s $125–145 billion and Google’s $190 billion figures are total-capex or infrastructure budgets, not the data\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: There is no market revenue consensus or company revenue guidance, so no quantified revenue reference sits on the ladder. NVIDIA's stated plan to launch in fall 2026 is not itself a revenue target and maps to no rung. The evidence supports a real product and customer interest, but the fall launch leaves a short FY2027 selling window; the customer signals are unscaled, and Wedbush's “billions of dollars in additional revenue” is neither period-specific nor limited to stand-alone CPU sales. Those signals improve the outlook but do not make at least $10 billion the most likely outcome.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"reasons\": \"There is no quantified market consensus or company revenue guidance, so the reference has no rung on this ladder. The fall 2026 launch leaves only a short selling window in FY2027; the new product and customer signals improve the outlook, but none gives shipment volumes, pricing, or a quantified FY2027 revenue estimate. Wedbush's “billions of dollars in additional revenue” is not period-specific and is not limited to stand-alone CPU sales. The evidence therefore still does not make $10 billion the most likely outcome.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e55\",\n    \"e285\",\n    \"e286\",\n    \"e306\",\n    \"e332\",\n    \"e333\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e55\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e285\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e286\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e306\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e332\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e333\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the evidence now includes the unveiled Vera CPU, Perplexity's stated integration and choice, and reports that NVIDIA has begun offering processors to cloud customers. These add concrete product and customer signals, changing the supporting facts without establishing revenue at or above $10 billion.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v11\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v12\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v4\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"No quantified market revenue consensus or company revenue guidance is supplied; therefore the reference has no rung on the ladder.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Stand-alone CPU revenue is most likely below $10 billion in FY2027 (rung 5). The fall launch implies a short selling window in the fiscal year, while no evidence quantifies shipments or pricing; the broad, non-period-specific “billions” claim does not establish $10 billion of stand-alone CPU revenue.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 11,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v7 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2028\\\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA's total revenue growth be in FY2028?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 60%. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 45% but below 60%. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 30% but below 45%. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 15% but below 30%. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 total revenue growth is below 15%.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 70 items]\\n[Not shown: 5 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e42 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Wall Street analysts project NVIDIA revenue growth of 82% in FY2027 and 41% in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e188; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e57 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Humanoid, LG Electronics, NEURA Robotics and Noble Machines 2026-07-06; from nvblog:open-models-icml-2026 2026-07-06] Humanoid, LG Electronics, NEURA Robotics and Noble Machines are adopting NVIDIA Isaac GR00T models for industrial humanoid deployments; no amount or timetable was given.\\n    Quote: Humanoid , LG Electronics , NEURA Robotics and Noble Machines are adopting NVIDIA Isaac GR00T models to accelerate industrial deployments of their humanoids\\n- e58 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source 1X, Agility, Agile Robots, Boston Dynamics, Hexagon Robotics and Mentee 2026-07-06; from nvblog:open-models-icml-2026 2026-07-06] 1X, Agility, Agile Robots, Boston Dynamics, Hexagon Robotics and Mentee are building humanoids using NVIDIA Cosmos world models, Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab; no amount or timetable was given.\\n    Quote: 1X , Agility , Agile Robots , Boston Dynamics , Hexagon Robotics , and Mentee are building the next generation of humanoids using Cosmos world models, Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab to accelerate the development and validation of their robots.\\n- e82 [third_party_view; fool.com analyst; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] Wall Street analysts expected NVIDIA revenue growth of 41% in 2027.\\n    Quote: For 2027, that figure rises to 41%.\\n- e87 [third_party_view; tradingview.com; original source Zacks ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] The Zacks Consensus Estimate calls for NVIDIA revenue and earnings to grow in FY2027 and FY2028; the article gives no estimate amounts.\\n    Quote: The Zacks Consensus Estimate points to continued revenue and earnings growth in both fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, suggesting that demand for NVIDIA's AI products remains far from slowing.\\n- e112 [fact; Autonocion.com; original source NVIDIA and Valar Atomics 2026-07-01; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] NVIDIA and Valar Atomics announced an agreement to explore nuclear-powered AI systems, including a 30-megawatt closed-loop AI factory in Utah; the project is under study and has no reported construction schedule.\\n    Quote: Alongside the demo, the two companies announced an agreement to jointly explore nuclear-powered AI systems. The concrete version under study is a 30-megawatt closed-loop AI factory in Utah, which Valar’s own announcement says “consumes no water from the local community.”\\n- e158 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St., quoting Lisa Su; original source Lisa Su, AMD CEO ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] AMD CEO Lisa Su said customer engagement for MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading-customer forecasts above AMD’s initial expectations.\\n    Quote: CEO Lisa Su has said “customer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations.”\\n- e159 [fact; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Broadcom reported Q2 AI semiconductor revenue of $10.8 billion, up 143% year over year, and guided Q3 revenue to $16.0 billion.\\n    Quote: Q2 AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion, +143% YoY, with Q3 guided to $16.0 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93, e344, e372; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e160 [fact; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Google ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google Cloud revenue rose 63%, while its backlog nearly doubled to more than $460 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion\\n- e162 [third_party_view; Reuters, as reported by mlq.ai; original source Analyst Richard Windey ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Analyst Richard Windey said U.S. export rules have effectively eliminated Nvidia's China revenue and limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China.\\n    Quote: Analyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e106, e167; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e163 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Industry surveys, as reported by mlq.ai ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to allocate 46% of AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, versus roughly 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134, e189, e347; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e189 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source Chinese executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Chinese executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they expect domestic products to receive 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e163; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e191 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Chinese government-led initiative, as reported by Bloomberg News ; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] A government-led initiative plans roughly 2 trillion yuan ($294 billion) of data-center construction in China over the next five years, with domestic companies expected to supply at least 80% of core technologies such as chips.\\n    Quote: China is allocating roughly 2 trillion yuan ($294 billion) to build data centers across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management. At least 80% of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e138; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e192 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence survey expects Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips become harder to find and local firms take over; Beijing has urged Chinese technology firms not to use H20 chips.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e193 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source The report (not further identified in the item) ; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The report says a global memory-chip shortage is likely to cap Chinese AI firms’ growth and benefit ChangXin Memory Technologies, as high-bandwidth memory supply becomes the bottleneck.\\n    Quote: But a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. with ChangXin Memory Technologies Inc. poised to reap the benefits. The bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e199 [fact; aibusiness.com; original source NVIDIA and Hugging Face 2026-07-06; from gnews:21e9182d07508a6690f10419 2026-07-08] NVIDIA and Hugging Face announced on July 6 that they had integrated NVIDIA’s Isaac GR00T 1.7 models and Isaac Teleop framework into LeRobot, with Cosmos 3 integration planned.\\n    Quote: Under the deal, unveiled on July 6, the companies added Nvidia's Isaac GR00T 1.7 open reasoning vision-language-action (VLA) models for humanoid robotics and the Isaac Teleop framework for robotics data collection into LeRobot, with the Cosmos 3 frontier robotics model planned for future integration.\\n- e200 [third_party_view; aibusiness.com; original source aibusiness.com ; from gnews:21e9182d07508a6690f10419 2026-07-08] Qualcomm’s Dragonwing IQ10 and Intel’s OpenVINO robotics offerings have recently entered the robotics market to challenge NVIDIA’s position.\\n    Quote: In addition, Qualcomm, with its Dragonwing IQ10 and Intel, with its OpenVINO robotics offerings, have recently entered the fold to challenge Nvidia’s position.\\n- e202 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA estimated HBM content per rack could increase by $0.2–$0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing could rise by $2–$3 million, from $3–$4 million for Blackwell to $6–$7 million for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: The report estimates HBM content per rack could rise by about $0.2 million to $0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing could increase by $2 million to $3 million, from roughly $3 million to $4 million for Blackwell to $6 million to $7 million for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e205 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA reported that NVIDIA's hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capital-expenditure growth, which it interpreted as wallet-share gains.\\n    Quote: The report also says Nvidia’s hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, suggesting wallet-share gains rather than share loss.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e196; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e206 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA modeled NVIDIA capturing roughly 65%–70% or more of AI capital spending over time, with the remainder going to ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n    Quote: BofA still models Nvidia holding roughly 65% to 70% plus of AI capex over time, with the rest split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n- e208 [rumour; Notebookcheck; original source Moore’s Law Is Dead, relaying an unnamed NVIDIA source ; from gnews:134fc6bce6a550f5376d6f27 2026-07-08] An unnamed NVIDIA source told Moore’s Law Is Dead that the RTX 60 series GPUs are not expected to launch in 2027.\\n    Quote: One of Moore’s Law Is Dead’s Nvidia source told the leaker that they don’t think the RTX 60 series GPUs are coming out in 2027.\\n- e209 [rumour; Notebookcheck; original source Notebookcheck ; from gnews:134fc6bce6a550f5376d6f27 2026-07-08] NVIDIA is reportedly planning to release RTX 50 Super series cards in 2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is reportedly planning to release the RTX 50 Super series cards in 2027.\\n- e210 [third_party_view; Notebookcheck; original source Notebookcheck ; from gnews:134fc6bce6a550f5376d6f27 2026-07-08] AMD is expected to release RDNA 5 gaming GPUs in 2027, a competitor product launch relevant to NVIDIA’s gaming GPU competition.\\n    Quote: AMD is expected to release the RDNA 5 gaming GPUs in 2027.\\n- e213 [third_party_view; TechCrunch author Russell Brandom; original source TechCrunch author Russell Brandom 2026-07-09; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] The author reports that memory companies increased high-bandwidth memory chip prices tenfold over the past year.\\n    Quote: they have been able to increase prices tenfold over the past year.\\n- e214 [third_party_view; TechCrunch author Russell Brandom; original source TechCrunch author Russell Brandom 2026-07-09; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] The author says the GPU shortage has eased somewhat compared with last year.\\n    Quote: The GPU shortage that looked so alarming last year has eased off a bit.\\n- e215 [third_party_view; TechCrunch author Russell Brandom; original source TechCrunch author Russell Brandom 2026-07-09; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] The author reports that the spot price for an hour of Nvidia H100 GPU compute peaked at about $3.20 in May 2026 and then steadily declined.\\n    Quote: Just like Nvidia’s stock price, there’s a peak in May (around $3.20 an hour) and then a steady drop-off.\\n- e216 [third_party_view; TechCrunch author Russell Brandom; original source TechCrunch author Russell Brandom 2026-07-09; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] The author reports that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and OpenAI have launched custom processors to reduce dependence on Nvidia, and says those chips are good enough to drive down compute prices.\\n    Quote: Google , Amazon , Microsoft , and even OpenAI have launched their own custom processors to lessen their dependence on Nvidia; even if those chips aren’t as good as the latest model from Nvidia, they’re good enough to drive down the price of compute.\\n- e217 [third_party_view; Ornn co-founder and CTO Wayne Nelms, quoted by TechCrunch author Russell Brandom; original source Wayne Nelms ; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] Ornn co-founder and CTO Wayne Nelms said more GPU and accelerator players are entering the market, while forecasting that current conditions will broadly persist absent an HBM breakthrough, a supply-demand shift, or a new memory entrant.\\n    Quote: “More GPU and accelerator players are entering the market. Everyone wants to make their own silicon, but no one is making their own DRAM,” Nelms told me. “Until there’s a major technological breakthrough on HBM [high-bandwidth memory], a shift in supply and demand, or someone new [enters the market in memory], I think things will more or less persist as we see today.”\\n- e229 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management said it had roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear customer commitments.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e400; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e235 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Bloomberg Intelligence projects NVIDIA will hold 70%–75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\\n    Quote: Bloomberg Intelligence projects the company will hold 70% to 75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\\n- e236 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Microsoft says its Maia 200 delivers 30% better performance per dollar than the fastest hardware in its fleet; the claim bears on competition with NVIDIA in AI chips.\\n    Quote: Microsoft says Maia 200 runs at 30% better performance per dollar than the fastest hardware already in its fleet.\\n- e237 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source qz.com ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta plans to build three more MTIA chip generations over the next two years for running generative AI models.\\n    Quote: Meta is set to build three more generations over the next two years with the purpose of running generative AI models.\\n- e238 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source qz.com ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta’s MTIA 450 is expected in early 2027, with twice the data-transfer speed of the prior generation.\\n    Quote: The next one, MTIA 450, arrives in early 2027, doubling how fast the chip moves data in and out of memory.\\n- e239 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source qz.com ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta’s MTIA 500 is expected later in 2027, with memory speed 50% higher than the MTIA 450.\\n    Quote: Its eventual successor, MTIA 500, is expected to come later next year, and pushes memory speed another 50% higher.\\n- e241 [fact; qz.com; original source Tesla ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla completed the AI5 inference-chip design in April 2026 and plans production at TSMC’s Arizona plant and Samsung’s Texas plant.\\n    Quote: Tesla finished the design of its AI5 inference chip in April 2026, according to Reuters . Production is planned at both TSMC 's plant in Arizona and Samsung's plant in Texas.\\n- e242 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Tesla ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla targets mid-2027 to begin mass production of its AI5 inference chip.\\n    Quote: Tesla is targeting mid-2027 for mass production.\\n- e243 [fact; qz.com; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] SpaceX filed plans in May for a Texas chip-manufacturing plant called Terafab, with stated planned cost of $55 billion and potential total investment of up to $119 billion.\\n    Quote: SpaceX, Musk's other property, filed plans in May for a $55 billion chip manufacturing plant in Texas called Terafab, Reuters reported . Total investment in the plant could reach $119 billion.\\n- e249 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta reportedly plans to begin manufacturing its AI chip in September; no year is specified.\\n    Quote: Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e265; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e250 [fact; moomoo.com; original source Quanta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Quanta's monthly sales rose to NT$385.19 billion in June, more than doubling year over year.\\n    Quote: Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\n- e251 [fact; moomoo.com; original source Hon Hai Precision Industry ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Hon Hai's monthly sales increased 52% year over year to NT$821.8 billion in June.\\n    Quote: $HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion\\n- e252 [fact; moomoo.com; original source Hon Hai Precision Industry ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Hon Hai's quarterly sales rose 40% year over year, above expectations; the quarter is not specified.\\n    Quote: allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\\n- e253 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source China ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] China is moving toward limited approval for domestic firms to buy NVIDIA H200 processors, with individual approval required for each requested quantity and use case.\\n    Quote: The stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\n- e254 [rumour; au.finance.yahoo.com; original source Cerebras 2026-07-09; from gnews:66cb0387229ac4b1390615cb 2026-07-09] Cerebras plans to bring its first European data-centre capacity online by the end of 2026, then expand across France and the Nordics; no capacity amount was given for this initial stage.\\n    Quote: US-based artificial intelligence chip maker Cerebras said on Thursday that it plans to bring its first European data centre capacity online by the end of 2026, followed by a rapid expansion across France and the Nordics, boosting its AI computing capacity in Europe.\\n- e255 [rumour; au.finance.yahoo.com; original source Cerebras ; from gnews:66cb0387229ac4b1390615cb 2026-07-09] Cerebras plans a European AI data-centre network with 200 MW of combined power capacity by the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: The company plans to build a network of AI data centres across Europe by the end of 2027, with a combined power capacity of 200 MW.\\n- e256 [party_statement; au.finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:66cb0387229ac4b1390615cb 2026-07-09] NVIDIA says its technology powers more than 90% of Europe's announced AI factory projects; the item gives no measurement date.\\n    Quote: Nvidia says its technology powers more than 90% of the continent's announced AI factory projects.\\n- e260 [third_party_view; The article; original source The article 2026-07-10; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] Alphabet and Amazon are increasingly deploying their own custom-made chips, adding competition for NVIDIA in data-center computing.\\n    Quote: For now, that competition — not just from AMD and Intel but also from its biggest customers like Alphabet Inc. and Amazon.com Inc., who are increasingly deploying their own custom-made chips — is a major factor holding back Nvidia’s stock price.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e145, e279; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e262 [third_party_view; Trefis; original source Wall Street consensus, as reported by Trefis ; from gnews:6abaf2856736877236d30f6a 2026-07-10] Trefis reports Wall Street consensus expects NVIDIA revenue to grow about 32% annually over the next few years.\\n    Quote: Wall Street consensus sees NVIDIA’s revenue growing about 32% a year for the next few years.\\n- e279 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com 2026-07-10; from gnews:4c201dc59ba0c7579bfef4db 2026-07-10] GuruFocus says major clients’ shift toward developing in-house AI chips could affect NVIDIA’s AI-market share.\\n    Quote: This strategic pivot could potentially impact NVIDIA's market share in the AI sector.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e260; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e280 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com 2026-07-10; from gnews:4c201dc59ba0c7579bfef4db 2026-07-10] GuruFocus reports analysts expect NVIDIA’s share within Meta to decline while major cloud providers’ overall AI capital expenditures rise significantly.\\n    Quote: However, analysts remain optimistic about NVIDIA's future, suggesting that while its share within Meta may decline, overall AI capital expenditures from major cloud service providers are expected to rise significantly.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e298 [rumour; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com, reporting unnamed rumors ; from gnews:170f44b2067e0405a5f5528a 2026-07-10] Recent rumors moved NVIDIA’s RTX 50 SUPER refresh debut from 2026 expectations to possibly late 2026 or CES 2027.\\n    Quote: Recent rumors have moved the refresh from 2026 expectations toward a possible late 2026 or CES 2027 debut\\n- e299 [rumour; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com, reporting an unnamed latest report ; from gnews:170f44b2067e0405a5f5528a 2026-07-10] An unnamed latest report said RTX 50 SUPER products were prepared and at least one board partner had received cards, but NVIDIA had put the lineup on hold while evaluating 3 GB GDDR7 memory pricing; no revised launch schedule was reported.\\n    Quote: the products are already prepared and that at least one board partner has received RTX 50 SUPER cards, but NVIDIA has placed them on hold while it evaluates the pricing of 3 GB GDDR7 memory. No revised launch schedule has reportedly been provided\\n- e300 [rumour; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com, reporting unnamed sources ; from gnews:170f44b2067e0405a5f5528a 2026-07-10] The article reported that 3 GB GDDR7 modules for the RTX 5070 Ti SUPER’s 24 GB configuration cost an estimated $60–$70 each, versus around $20 for current 2 GB modules.\\n    Quote: the 3 GB GDDR7 modules required for its 24 GB configuration reportedly cost between $60 and $70 each, compared to around $20 for current 2 GB modules\\n- e317 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool; original source Keithen Drury 2026-07-12; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] The author says Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta, Anthropic, and OpenAI in late 2026 and into 2027, with new clients launching chips next year.\\n    Quote: Broadcom expects major growth next year as new clients launch their custom AI chips. Currently, Broadcom's major customer is Alphabet , with its Tensor Processing Units (TPUs). However, Broadcom is also making custom AI chips for Meta Platforms , Anthropic, and OpenAI, and production for those chips is scheduled to ramp up late this year and into next.\\n- e330 [party_statement; tech-insider.org; original source NVIDIA, reporting AWS's plan ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] AWS planned to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs during 2027–2028, according to NVIDIA's August 2026 statement.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\\n- e337 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix reported that quarterly revenue grew 198% to about $35 billion, a record.\\n    Quote: In the recent quarter, for example, the company reported revenue growth of 198% to about $35 billion dollars, a record level.\\n- e338 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix reported that quarterly net income rose 398% and operating margin was 72%.\\n    Quote: Net income soared 398%, and operating margin came in at 72%.\\n- e339 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Counterpoint Research ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] Counterpoint Research said SK Hynix held 58% of the global high-bandwidth memory market at the end of the first quarter.\\n    Quote: SK Hynix held 58% share globally of the HBM market as of the end of the first quarter, according to Counterpoint Research.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e289; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e340 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix and NVIDIA ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix and NVIDIA recently signed a multi-year technology partnership under which SK Hynix will supply memory for areas including AI infrastructure, personal AI and robotics.\\n    Quote: The two companies even reinforced their relationship recently, signing a multi-year technology partnership. It involves SK Hynix supplying memory across a broad range of areas, from AI infrastructure to personal AI and robotics .\\n- e341 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted by fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said SK Hynix has been and will continue to be NVIDIA's largest memory partner.\\n    Quote: \\\"SK Hynix has been Nvidia's largest memory partner and will continue to be our largest memory partner,\\\" Nvidia chief Jensen Huang said at the time.\\n- e360 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Google ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Google has approached neocloud companies about using its TPUs, according to The Information; no date or amount is given.\\n    Quote: Google has approached so-called neocloud companies about using its tensor processing units, or TPUs, according to a report from The Information.\\n- e361 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nscale ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Nscale reportedly told Google that its active projects remain centered on GPU capacity; no date or amount is given.\\n    Quote: One provider, Nscale, reportedly told Google that its active projects remain centered on GPU capacity.\\n- e362 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Google and Blackstone ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Google is working with Blackstone on a new cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms in 2027.\\n    Quote: Google is also working with Blackstone on a new cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms next year.\\n- e393 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, reporting Milk Road AI’s estimate; original source Milk Road AI ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Milk Road AI estimates that one GW of AI infrastructure costs $6 billion–$11 billion with Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks, about $19 billion with Nvidia GB300-based racks, and $25 billion with Vera Rubin racks.\\n    Quote: Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks. Under Vera Rubin, rack costs jump to $25 billion.\\n- e399 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Goldman Sachs estimates AI compute spending at approximately $494 billion in 2026, rising to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion this year to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\\n- e400 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said Blackwell and Rubin platform revenue would total at least $1 trillion through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, CEO Jensen Huang has said that he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from Nvidia's Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e229; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e401 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of approximately $555 billion in FY2028, ending January 2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia will generate approximately $555 billion in revenue for the company's next fiscal year, ending January 2028.\\n- e407 [party_statement; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source Nokia 2026-07-15; from gnews:e153ff10e5adda87a52745a4 2026-07-14] Nokia said the Nvidia-powered AI radio-access-network hardware is expected to become available to operators in 2027.\\n    Quote: Next year, when the hardware will become available to operators, spectrum efficiency gains are expected to reach 50%, while currently 20% have been achieved.\\n- e408 [third_party_view; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source sg.finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:e153ff10e5adda87a52745a4 2026-07-14] Ericsson is upgrading its mobile-network equipment in anticipation of more AI-powered devices running on mobile networks.\\n    Quote: Swedish competitor Ericsson AB is also upgrading its equipment in anticipation of more AI-powered devices, such as robots, running on mobile networks.\\n- e450 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe; original source Airlangga Hartarto, Indonesia Chief Economic Affairs Minister ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Indonesia Chief Economic Affairs Minister Airlangga Hartarto said investors are planning about 1.3 GW of data-center capacity, in addition to about 580 MW already operating; no period was stated.\\n    Quote: Chief Economic Affairs Minister Airlangga Hartarto said investors are planning data center projects with a combined capacity of about 1.3 gigawatts (GW), on top of the roughly 580 megawatts (MW) currently in operation.\\n- e453 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 to provide AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Quote: Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v11 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus is supplied. The specific report that China plans to permit the three named firms, together with Reuters’ report of progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon o\\n- v12 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much incremental compute capacity will Google, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft add in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 15 to under 20 GW; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: No consensus or company guidance is supplied. The only quantified market expectation shown is Morgan Stanley’s 19.5 GW forecast, which sits in rung 3. That forecast also implies rung 3: 19.5 GW is at least 15 GW but below 20 GW, so it does not cross into rung 4. The 2025 figure is historical context\\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around the reported $650 billion plan; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence does not establish a quantified change to the four companies’ 2026 data-center spending: Meta’s $125–145 billion and Google’s $190 billion figures are total-capex or infrastructure budgets, not the data\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The FY2028 Wall Street estimate of 41% is in rung 3, as is the broader estimate of about 32% annual growth over the next few years. New evidence of strong NVIDIA hyperscaler sales and continued AI infrastructure demand nudges the estimate modestly above the prior 42% assessment. That support is tempered by China-related limits, rising custom-chip competition, and falling H100 compute prices. The implied estimate remains below the 45% rung-2 boundary, so the most likely outcome stays in rung 3.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The direct market reference is 41% FY2028 revenue growth, which sits in rung 3; the broader estimate of about 32% annually also sits in rung 3. New evidence of strong hyperscaler sales and sizable planned NVIDIA GPU deployments nudges the forecast above the 41% reference. However, those deployments are spread across periods and are not fully quantified as NVIDIA FY2028 revenue, while custom-chip competition, lower compute prices, China-related headwinds, and HBM constraints offset some of the demand support. The implied growth remains below the 45% boundary for rung 2.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e42\",\n    \"e262\",\n    \"e401\",\n    \"e205\",\n    \"e229\",\n    \"e330\",\n    \"e453\",\n    \"e250\",\n    \"e251\",\n    \"e206\",\n    \"e162\",\n    \"e163\",\n    \"e191\",\n    \"e192\",\n    \"e216\",\n    \"e215\",\n    \"e217\",\n    \"e214\",\n    \"e317\",\n    \"e360\",\n    \"e362\",\n    \"e253\",\n    \"e202\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e42\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e262\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e401\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e205\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e229\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e330\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e453\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e250\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e251\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e206\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e162\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e163\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e191\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e192\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e216\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e215\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e217\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e214\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e317\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e360\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e362\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e253\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e202\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 3, but the implied growth estimate moves modestly from the previous 42% assessment to about 43% with the newly shown deployment and demand evidence; the updated China-policy theme also adds upside. These changes do not lift the forecast to 45%.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v11\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v12\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v4\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"Wall Street's FY2028 growth estimate of 41% (e42) is the direct reference and sits in rung 3. The approximately $555 billion FY2028 revenue estimate (e401) is a revenue level, not a separate growth-rate forecast; the broader ~32% annual estimate (e262) is also in rung 3.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About 43% FY2028 revenue growth: start from the direct 41% estimate, then make a modest upward adjustment for strong hyperscaler sales and planned GPU deployments, tempered by competition, pricing, supply, and China risks. The deployments span periods and are not all sized as NVIDIA FY2028 revenue, so they do not support taking the full upside.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 12,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v5 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \\\"A U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security finding, penalty, or new export-control measure stemming from the investigation into DeepSeek's access to restricted Nvidia chips.\\\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will the U.S. Commerce Department or Bureau of Industry and Security publicly issue a finding, impose a penalty, or announce a new export-control measure stemming from its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips by 2027-01-31?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: The U.S. Commerce Department or Bureau of Industry and Security publicly issues a finding, imposes a penalty, or announces a new export-control measure stemming from the investigation by 2027-01-31. / rung 2 Ruled out: The U.S. Commerce Department or Bureau of Industry and Security formally abandons or withdraws the investigation without issuing a finding, imposing a penalty, or announcing a new export-control measure stemming from it. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 9 items]\\n- e67 [fact; The Diplomat; original source U.S. Commerce Department / U.S. government ; from gnews:1eeceeb212a2c82c2e75e806 2026-07-02] The U.S. Commerce Department began investigations in February 2025 into whether Chinese AI company DeepSeek gained access to restricted Nvidia AI chips, including whether the chips were sourced from third parties in Malaysia and Singapore, and the U.S. government is probing whether DeepSeek built its R1 AI model on restricted Nvidia chips. The item also states that U.S. export controls on the most sophisticated AI chips and chipmaking tools, imposed by President Joe Biden, remain in force and aim to limit China's chip and AI development.\\n    Quote: The Singaporean investigation is an attempt to plug the holes in the regime of export controls that U.S. President Joe Biden placed on the most sophisticated AI chips, as well as the tools used to produce them. The goal of these restrictions was to hamstring China’s efforts to develop its own chip-making industry and prevent it from developing AI capabilities that could give the country a military\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e222; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e68 [fact; CommonWealth Magazine; original source Singapore authorities and prosecutors ; from gnews:3345f0d91321165a099860c9 2026-07-02] Singapore authorities seized a luxury home valued at about SGD 55 million (US$42 million) under a prohibition-of-disposal order and seized about SGD 1 million in bank accounts under investigation, as part of a case of suspected cross-border smuggling of hardware containing Nvidia chips. Two individuals, Lim Jenny and Woon Guo Jie Aaron, face money-laundering charges; four firms — Aperia International, A-Speed Infotech and Aperia Cloud Services (II) (the Aperia group) and Luxuriate Your Life — face charges of false representation over the alleged export of computer servers that might contain Nvidia chips to Malaysia. Luxuriate Your Life was charged on July 1, the other three firms on July 6; three individuals were also charged on July 1 with offenses including fraud and money laundering. The item states the case follows a US investigation into whether Chinese start-up DeepSeek circumvented US restrictions on advanced Nvidia chips by buying them from third parties in other countries, including Singapore.\\n    Quote: Singapore authorities have seized a multimillion-dollar mansion and charged people with money laundering as part of a case of suspected cross-border smuggling of hardware containing Nvidia chips. … A prohibition of disposal order was issued against the luxury home, valued at about SGD 55M ($42M), freezing the asset and preventing its purchase or sale. Funds in bank accounts under investigation, va\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e32; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e176 [fact; The Straits Times; original source Singapore authorities (charges); conduct attributed to Alan Wei Zhaolun and Aperia International, Aperia Cloud Services (II) and A-Speed Infotech 2026-07-06; from gnews:f0d529209a91c7a237e117f1 2026-07-06] On 6 July 2026, Singapore authorities handed Alan Wei Zhaolun nine additional charges — three counts of money laundering and six of fraud — bringing his total to 11, and separately charged three Aperia-linked companies with fraud (Aperia International five counts, Aperia Cloud Services (II) two, A-Speed Infotech one). The fraud charges allege a conspiracy, in 2023–2024, to falsely represent to server suppliers Dell, Super Micro Computer and Asus that an Aperia Group company would be the end-user of the servers. The case arose after Singapore came under scrutiny in a US investigation into whether DeepSeek circumvented US restrictions on advanced Nvidia chips by buying them through third parties abroad; Wei, Woon and Li were first brought to court in February 2025.\\n    Quote: On July 6, Alan Wei Zhaolun, 50, was handed nine additional charges – three counts of money laundering and six counts of fraud. … According to his latest charges, he also allegedly conspired with Aaron Woon Guo Jie, 41, and Jenny Lim, 51, to commit fraud multiple times in 2023 and 2024. … The police said the fraud charges allege that the trio engaged in a conspiracy to commit fraud linked to the p\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e101; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e102 (2026-07-06); both are in force: Singaporean prosecutors alleged that Alan Wei Zhaolun and other Aperia Group executives defrauded Dell, Super Micro and Asustek by misrepresenting the actual end-users of hardware that may have contained Nvidia AI processors subject to US export controls; between November 2023 and February 2025, three Aperia executives falsely told the suppliers that one of the Aperia Group's companies would be the end-user of the servers, according to a police statement published July 1.\\n- e220 [fact; e.vnexpress.net (citing Channel News Asia); original source Singapore Police Force 2026-07-01; from gnews:c6445931955f107b551cce85 2026-07-07] Singapore police issued a prohibition of disposal order on July 1, 2026 freezing any sale or transfer of a S$55 million (US$42.5 million) Good Class Bungalow at 12 Chee Hoon Avenue near the Singapore Botanic Gardens, allegedly bought with proceeds of a scheme to smuggle servers believed to contain Nvidia AI chips that Washington bars from export to China; officers also seized about S$1 million from bank accounts tied to the case.\\n    Quote: Singapore police have seized a S$55 million (US$42.5 million) luxury bungalow allegedly bought with the proceeds of a scheme to smuggle servers believed to contain Nvidia artificial intelligence chips that Washington bars from export to China. … The Singapore Police Force issued a prohibition of disposal order on July 1 freezing any sale or transfer of the Good Class Bungalow, the city-state's mos\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e32; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e221 [party_statement; e.vnexpress.net; original source Singapore prosecutors ; from gnews:c6445931955f107b551cce85 2026-07-07] Prosecutors allege that Aperia Group chief executive Wei Zhaolun and two other Aperia executives deceived Dell, Super Micro Computer and Asus between November 2023 and February 2025 by certifying on end-user forms that companies within the group would receive the servers — servers the article says were believed to contain Nvidia AI chips barred from export to China.\\n    Quote: Singapore police have seized a S$55 million (US$42.5 million) luxury bungalow allegedly bought with the proceeds of a scheme to smuggle servers believed to contain Nvidia artificial intelligence chips that Washington bars from export to China. … Prosecutors say Wei and two other Aperia executives deceived Dell, Super Micro Computer and Asus between November 2023 and February 2025 by certifying on \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e102; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e222 [third_party_view; e.vnexpress.net; original source The Diplomat (reporting a U.S. Commerce Department inquiry) ; from gnews:c6445931955f107b551cce85 2026-07-07] The article reports that the Singapore smuggling case grew out of U.S. investigations into whether Chinese AI firm DeepSeek obtained restricted Nvidia chips through intermediaries in Singapore and Malaysia, a Commerce Department inquiry reported by The Diplomat; Singapore police have not confirmed any link between Aperia and DeepSeek.\\n    Quote: The case grew out of U.S. investigations into whether Chinese AI firm DeepSeek obtained restricted Nvidia chips through intermediaries in Singapore and Malaysia, a Commerce Department inquiry reported by The Diplomat . … Singapore police have not confirmed any link between Aperia and DeepSeek.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e67; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e223 [party_statement; e.vnexpress.net (citing Malay Mail); original source K. Shanmugam, Singapore Home Affairs and Law Minister ; from gnews:c6445931955f107b551cce85 2026-07-07] Singapore Home Affairs and Law Minister K. Shanmugam said, when the first charges were laid in March 2025, that servers in the case were routed through Singapore and shipped on to Malaysia; whether Malaysia was the final destination or a further transit point remains unconfirmed, and some reporting points to China.\\n    Quote: The servers were routed through Singapore and shipped on to Malaysia, Home Affairs and Law Minister K. Shanmugam said when the first charges were laid in March 2025, according to Malay Mail . … Whether Malaysia was the final destination or a further transit point remains unconfirmed, and some reporting points to China.\\n- e499 [rumour; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware, citing the Financial Times and unnamed sources; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] Nvidia has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to stop its AI chips and servers being smuggled into China, cutting the number of authorized clients by more than half, according to the Financial Times; remaining clients passed tougher compliance inspections to confirm they are genuine businesses rather than shell companies. Nvidia's reported steps included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification and interviewing end users. Sources told the FT Nvidia made the move after Washington pressured it to tighten its legal compliance.\\n    Quote: AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they a\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e544; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e502 [fact; Financial Times, via Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. law enforcement (arrest), Singapore and Taiwan authorities (seizure and raids), as reported by the Financial Times ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] According to the Financial Times, Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw was arrested alongside two other suspects for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China; Singapore seized a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers, and Taiwan raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip-smuggling probe.\\n    Quote: which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China. … This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-cha\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e32; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is rung 3: the supplied record describes an investigation but gives no market consensus or U.S. decision deadline, and no U.S. outcome has been announced. The Singapore charges and asset seizures add allegations about a possible chip-smuggling route, making a U.S. outcome somewhat more plausible, but they do not establish a DeepSeek link or a U.S. decision, timetable, or formal abandonment. The evidence therefore does not make either rung 1 or rung 2 the most likely outcome by the due date.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is rung 3: no market consensus, U.S. guidance, or stated Commerce/BIS decision deadline is supplied, so the reference remains an unresolved outcome by the due date. The evidence makes a U.S. action more plausible: the investigation is reported, and Singapore charges and broader chip-smuggling enforcement add relevant context. But those developments do not provide a U.S. finding, penalty, new measure, or timetable tied to the DeepSeek investigation; nor is there evidence of formal abandonment. The increased likelihood of action does not make it the most likely outcome by 2027-01-31, so the forecast remains rung 3.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e67\",\n    \"e68\",\n    \"e176\",\n    \"e220\",\n    \"e221\",\n    \"e222\",\n    \"e223\",\n    \"e499\",\n    \"e502\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e67\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e68\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e176\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e220\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e221\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e222\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e223\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e499\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e502\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung is unchanged, but recent evidence adds specific Singapore charges and broader alleged Nvidia-chip smuggling enforcement, including an arrest, which raises the forecast likelihood of U.S. action compared with the prior judgement. It still does not supply a U.S. decision or a decision timetable tied to DeepSeek.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"Rung 3: no market consensus, U.S. guidance, or stated Commerce/BIS decision deadline is supplied; the reference is therefore an unresolved outcome by 2027-01-31.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Most likely, neither a public Commerce/BIS finding, penalty, or new export-control measure stemming from the investigation nor a formal abandonment will have occurred by 2027-01-31; the matter will remain unresolved. This reflects the reported investigation and relevant enforcement developments, discounted because they do not establish a U.S. action or timetable tied to DeepSeek.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 13,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The amount of NVIDIA's credit-support obligations under the announced AI-cloud programme.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud programme by its next annual report?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: NVIDIA reports no commitments under the programme. / rung 2 Bullish: Commitments total more than $0 and less than $1 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Commitments total at least $1 billion and less than $5 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Commitments total at least $5 billion and less than $10 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Commitments total at least $10 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 23 items]\\n[Not shown: 37 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e1 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced a partnership model with AI clouds that combines revenue sharing with credit support to deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3, e19, e31, e98; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2 [rumour; moomoo.com, citing The Information; original source The Information 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] The Information reported on 2026-07-01 that NVIDIA was providing young GPU cloud providers financial guarantees, including repurchasing their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price if they failed to secure enough customers.\\n    Quote: On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\\n- e3 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced on 2026-07-01 a business model combining revenue sharing and credit support for AI cloud providers procuring NVIDIA infrastructure.\\n    Quote: On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1, e10, e35; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e5 [rumour; moomoo.com, citing The Information and three executives; original source The Information 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus and Sharon AI have joined NVIDIA’s reported AI cloud-provider program, according to The Information and three executives with existing NVIDIA business ties.\\n    Quote: GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e9 [fact; moomoo.com, citing NVIDIA’s regulatory filing; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] NVIDIA disclosed in a filing submitted in May 2026 that it had added $3.5 billion of guarantees for customer data-center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares; the filing covered the quarter ended in April 2026.\\n    Quote: According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\\n- e13 [fact; qz.com; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies plans a campus in Batam, Indonesia, with capacity of up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Quote: A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e7, e22, e28, e33, e99; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e20 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Under the backstop, NVIDIA agrees to rent back unused GPUs from AI-cloud customers at a fixed rate.\\n    Quote: The backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate.\\n- e21 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com; original source Firmus and Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to adopt NVIDIA's AI-cloud backstop offer.\\n    Quote: Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e22 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia.\\n    Quote: Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia,\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e27 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed a six-year agreement covering 72 MW of new Australian data-center capacity, with capacity for up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e28 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 MW and hold up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13, e38, e46; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e35 [fact; mlq.ai; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] NVIDIA unveiled the AI Compute Partnership on July 1, providing a financial backstop for neocloud GPU deployments in exchange for a recurring share of cloud revenue generated on that capacity; the amount of support was not disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3, e60; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e62 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says NVIDIA’s backstops typically run for six years; its illustrative lower-end pricing curve averages $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years, and it expects most neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative\\n- e66 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI announced a 72MW Australian AI factory backed by NVIDIA for up to 40,000 GB300 GPUs over six years, disclosing $4.88 billion of backstop value, or an implied average floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour.\\n    Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\\n- e67 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI guides to a total fleet of more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs by mid-2027, including up to 40,000 backstopped GB300s; its footprint is set to reach 132MW, of which 102MW is contracted.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\\n- e68 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced its 360MW Batam AI cluster project on June 29, 2026, as part of NVIDIA’s backstop program.\\n    Quote: Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\\n- e69 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years for the Batam cluster project.\\n    Quote: Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years.\\n- e100 [third_party_view; futurumgroup.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Firmus has $25–30 billion in committed off-take, though the author says whether it converts to recognized revenue on schedule remains uncertain.\\n    Quote: Whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n- e179 [party_statement; Futuriom; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA said Sharon AI signed onto its AI-cloud plan and will buy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA states that two AI cloud providers have already signed on for the new deal: Sharon AI, based in Australia, will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e37; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e219 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:86c243945145ed901feb26cf 2026-07-09] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA faced gross-margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, and concerns about cash use in vendor financing rather than buybacks or dividends.\\n    Quote: Several concerns are fueling the sell-off, Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya wrote in a note this week. They include gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks/dividends.\\n- e451 [fact; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies and DayOne ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus Technologies and DayOne are developing NVIDIA's DSX AI Factory Campus in Batam, a planned 360 MW AI data-center complex.\\n    Quote: The Batam development marks Firmus Technologies' first data center investment in Indonesia. The Australian AI infrastructure company is partnering with Singapore-based data center operator DayOne to develop the Nvidia DSX AI Factory Campus, a 360-megawatt AI data center complex that is expected to become one of Southeast Asia's largest AI computing hubs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e46; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e452 [third_party_view; Jakarta Globe, citing The Straits Times; original source The Straits Times ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] The Straits Times projected that the Batam campus could generate up to $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n    Quote: According to The Straits Times , the project is projected to generate as much as $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n- e453 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 to provide AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Quote: Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v11 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus is supplied. The specific report that China plans to permit the three named firms, together with Reuters’ report of progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon o\\n- v12 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much incremental compute capacity will Google, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft add in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 15 to under 20 GW; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: No consensus or company guidance is supplied. The only quantified market expectation shown is Morgan Stanley’s 19.5 GW forecast, which sits in rung 3. That forecast also implies rung 3: 19.5 GW is at least 15 GW but below 20 GW, so it does not cross into rung 4. The 2025 figure is historical context\\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around the reported $650 billion plan; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence does not establish a quantified change to the four companies’ 2026 data-center spending: Meta’s $125–145 billion and Google’s $190 billion figures are total-capex or infrastructure budgets, not the data\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The company’s announcement of positive but unquantified credit support is the reference, at rung 2 under the ladder convention; no quantified market consensus is supplied. Sharon AI’s disclosed $4.88 billion backstop is already a substantial commitment. Applying its approximately $122,000 per-GPU backstop value to Firmus’s planned 170,000 GPUs implies about $20.7 billion for Firmus. The report of $25–30 billion in committed off-take makes the project’s volume more credible than a bare capacity target, although delivery timing remains uncertain. Adding the Sharon and Firmus estimates gives about $25.6 billion, above the $10 billion rung-5 boundary; this is an estimate of total multi-year backstop value, not an annualized figure.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 4,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s positive but unquantified announcement of credit support, which sits at rung 2 under the ladder convention. The implied amount is about $9 billion by the next annual report: Sharon AI’s disclosed $4.88 billion backstop, plus an estimated roughly $4.1 billion for Firmus. That Firmus estimate applies Sharon’s approximately $122,000 per-GPU backstop value to 170,000 GPUs, then discounts the result to about one-fifth because Firmus’s deployment is scheduled in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 and the report is due in February 2027. The estimate is above rung 3’s $5 billion boundary but below rung 5’s $10 billion boundary. The whole-project, undiscounted extrapolation would be much higher, but the evidence does not establish that Firmus’s full planned volume will be committed by the due date.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e1\",\n    \"e2\",\n    \"e3\",\n    \"e5\",\n    \"e13\",\n    \"e20\",\n    \"e21\",\n    \"e22\",\n    \"e27\",\n    \"e28\",\n    \"e35\",\n    \"e62\",\n    \"e66\",\n    \"e67\",\n    \"e68\",\n    \"e69\",\n    \"e100\",\n    \"e179\",\n    \"e451\",\n    \"e452\",\n    \"e453\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e2\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e3\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e9\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e13\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e20\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e21\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e22\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e27\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e28\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e35\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e62\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e66\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e67\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e68\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e69\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e100\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e179\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e219\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e451\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e452\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e453\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The prior rung-5 estimate treated Firmus’s full planned 170,000-GPU capacity as support committed by the report date. The newly specified phased deployment schedule changes the implied amount for that date; discounting the Firmus estimate brings the total to about $9 billion and rung 4.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v12\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v4\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA’s announced positive but unquantified credit-support model is the reference; under the ladder convention it sits at rung 2 (more than $0 and less than $1 billion).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About $9 billion: Sharon AI’s $4.88 billion disclosed backstop plus roughly $4.1 billion for Firmus, using Sharon’s approximately $122,000 per-GPU value, Firmus’s 170,000-GPU plan, and a roughly one-fifth timing discount for the phased rollout through early 2028.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 14,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"The sum of calendar-2026 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, versus the roughly $650 billion they are reported to plan for this year.\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How much did the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures during calendar 2026, according to their full-year 2026 capex disclosures?\\nLadder: rung 1 below 500,000,000,000 USD: below 500,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD: from 500,000,000,000 USD to below 600,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD: from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year\\\") / rung 4 from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD: from 700,000,000,000 USD to below 800,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 800,000,000,000 USD or more: 800,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 26 items]\\n- e82 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article author; original source The big four AI hyperscalers ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in the current year (2026). This is a planned spending level by the customer group, not a committed order for NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e196; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e83 [party_statement; fool.com (Motley Fool) article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (2027), up from the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026. This is NVIDIA's own projection, not a reported outcome.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e125, e197, e205; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e55 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom's Hardware reported that public opposition to data centers — blamed for large increases in power/utility costs and water consumption, with 7 in 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026, a demand signal for AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: This has become a major national issue recently, with data centers being blamed for massive increases in power and utility costs, as well as increased water consumption and a reduction in the quality of life in the communities that surround these developments. … These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e125 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] On its most recent quarterly conference call, NVIDIA management forecast that hyperscaler spending would top $1 trillion in 2027, after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e83; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e158 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. The spending spans chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking required to run AI at scale.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\\n- e161 [third_party_view; autonocion.com, citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] More than 75 data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom's Hardware, and polling cited in the same reporting found 7 in 10 Americans oppose a data center built near their home.\\n    Quote: With over $130 billion in data center projects already blocked or delayed by communities fed up with power and water demands, a zero-local-water nuclear option isn't just an engineering story — it's a potential unlock for the entire AI buildout. … More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e55; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e170 [party_statement; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein, reported by Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (management) ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Hon Hai Precision Industry, one of Nvidia's largest suppliers, reported strong earnings on the Friday before the 2026-07-06 article and its management forecast a continued acceleration in AI demand; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein called this \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\" No figures for the results were given.\\n    Quote: The analyst additionally pointed to strong earnings last Friday from Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (TW:2317), one of Nvidia's largest suppliers. Klein noted that Hon Hai management forecasted a continued acceleration in AI demand, which the analyst said was \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\"\\n- e196 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source fool.com author (aggregating the big four AI hyperscalers' capex plans) ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The article states that in calendar 2026 the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures.\\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e82; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e205 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source AI hyperscalers (capex plans; 2027 forecast is the author's) ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e83; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e215 [fact; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Google ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported that Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditure up to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\\n- e250 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by Futuriom); original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported (July 1) that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models, on the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model; the article notes Meta had planned to buy 'millions' of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year and questions what its move implies for AI demand.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\\n- e256 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings, a data-center electrical and technology systems contractor, reported March-quarter 2026 results: Communications segment revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which it attributed to continued strong demand in the data center market; Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million; and total backlog rose 62% since the end of fiscal 2025 to $3.86 billion, driven by customer demand and expansion capacity.\\n    Quote: Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” adding that “the recent capital investments we have made have positioned us well to respond to that demand and deliver solutions to our customers.” … Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million, and total backlog exploded to $3.86 \\n- e257 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services, a grid and electrical infrastructure contractor, reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus, and backlog that rose to a record $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $34.70–35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55–14.25 in adjusted EPS, and cited a path to more than double adjusted EPS by 2030.\\n    Quote: Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion. Management raised full-year 2026 guidance to a range of $34.70 billion to $35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55 to $14.25 in adjusted EPS.\\n- e258 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA, a mechanical/HVAC contractor, reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year (51% organic), diluted EPS of $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus, and backlog of $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion a year earlier; data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a year earlier. … Data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue\\n- e259 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA (CFO Bill George) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] On the Q1 2026 call, Comfort Systems USA finance chief Bill George said the constraint on the business is now a supply (labor) issue rather than demand, saying 'There is plenty more work we could take if we could possibly do it' — indicating data-center work demand exceeds the contractor's capacity to execute.\\n    Quote: Today, I think it’s really important for people to understand that it’s a supply issue. There is plenty more work we could take if we could possibly do it.\\n- e260 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 diluted EPS of $6.84 versus $5.90 consensus, Remaining Performance Obligations at a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50–19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25–29.75.\\n    Quote: Diluted EPS of $6.84 beat the $5.90 consensus by 15.85%, Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50 billion to $19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25 to $29.75.\\n- e261 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 (beating consensus by a double-digit percentage), revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin; it raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 crushed the consensus by a double-digit percentage, revenue jumped 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue rocketed 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin. Management raised the full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40 to $19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n- e353 [fact; Reuters (reported by gurufocus.com); original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:09b9d76297a57f198651c5e7 2026-07-10] Reuters reported that Meta Platforms plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom and manufactured by TSMC, and the report said it is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing, rather than replacing, the large number of graphics processors Meta continues to buy from Nvidia and Advanced Micro Devices.\\n    Quote: Reuters said Meta plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom (AVGO) and manufactured by Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSM). The report said the processor is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing, rather than replacing, the large number of graphics pro\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e336; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e445 [fact; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, including AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion that grew 143%, as reported by 247wallst.com analyst Alex Sirois.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q2 FY2026 revenue hit $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, with AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e489; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e449 [party_statement; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom management has telegraphed $16.0 billion of AI revenue for its Q3, roughly 54% of total sales, ahead of its Q3 report scheduled for September 8, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Q3 earnings report lands September 8, and management has already telegraphed $16.0 billion in AI revenue, roughly 54% of total sales.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e467 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (Alex Sirois); original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to $125-$145 billion, the article reports.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion\\n- e475 [party_statement; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon (the big four hyperscalers) ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers — Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon — are planning higher capital expenditures in 2026. No individual capex figures are given. Reported by Justin Pope of The Motley Fool.\\n    Quote: Hyperscalers, including Meta Platforms , Microsoft , Alphabet , and Amazon , are planning higher capital expenditures in 2026.\\n- e476 [third_party_view; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Goldman Sachs estimates that industry AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion in 2026 to $1.13 trillion by 2031, as reported by The Motley Fool's Justin Pope. This is a broader industry demand forecast, not a figure for NVIDIA or for the big four's data-center capex specifically.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion this year to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\\n- e496 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source Meta ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] Meta's $145 billion infrastructure budget, aimed partly at custom silicon, was cited among competitive threats facing NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The Reddit crowd has fixated on competitive threats, from DeepSeek’s rumored in-house chip to Meta’s $145B infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon.\\n- e514 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Broadcom (Hock Tan, CEO) ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan said momentum continues, with fiscal Q3 AI semiconductor revenue set to grow more than 200% year over year to $16 billion; Broadcom guided total Q3 revenue to $29.4 billion (+84% YoY), set a stated goal of exceeding $100 billion in AI sales by 2027, and reported AI semiconductor revenue of $10.8 billion last quarter, up 143% YoY — custom AI silicon revenue that competes for the same AI data-center spending as NVIDIA, per 247wallst.com's 2026-07-14 article.\\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Hock Tan says “the momentum continues” with Q3 AI semi revenue set to grow over 200% year-over-year to $16 billion. … Broadcom guided Q3 revenue to $29.4B (+84% YoY), and management’s stated goal is to exceed $100B in AI sales by 2027. AI semi revenue grew 143% YoY to $10.8B last quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e535 [fact; EBC (ebc.com); original source Amazon.com Inc. ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Amazon recently borrowed another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt, as investors question hyperscaler returns on their large AI hardware spending, EBC reported.\\n    Quote: Amazon recently made headlines by borrowing another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt . … These tech behemoths have spent hundreds of billions of dollars purchasing Nvidia's hardware, but Wall Street is starting to demand proof of return on investment (ROI).\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is the big four's reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). Meta's newly raised $125–$145 billion 2026 capex plan and Google's up-to-$190 billion capex guidance add upward pressure, but neither states the data-center portion or provides enough information to calculate a change to the group's roughly $650 billion data-center plan. Demand and supplier signals are broadly supportive, while delayed projects are a downside risk; neither side quantifies a change to the big four's 2026 spend. The evidence therefore still implies about $650 billion, within rung 3, and does not support crossing either the $600 billion or $700 billion boundary.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). Meta's higher 2026 capex plan, Google's up-to-$190 billion capex guidance, and the report that the big four are planning higher capex all lean upward, but none quantifies the change to the group's data-center-only spending. Delayed projects and execution constraints lean the other way, but do not quantify a reduction in the big four's 2026 spend. The evidence therefore leaves the most likely total within rung 3; it does not support crossing either the $600 billion or $700 billion boundary.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e82\",\n    \"e215\",\n    \"e467\",\n    \"e475\",\n    \"e161\",\n    \"e259\",\n    \"e170\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e82\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e215\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e467\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e475\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e161\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e259\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e170\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The latest evidence does not change the key quantified basis for the forecast: the roughly $650 billion group data-center plan remains the reference, while the newer capex and demand signals do not provide a data-center-specific group revision. The project-delay figure and labor constraint do not translate into a quantified reduction in this group's 2026 spend.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"Around $650 billion planned for 2026; this reference sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About $650 billion, carried forward from the reported group data-center plan. The newer company capex figures are not data-center-only, and the other signals do not quantify a change to the combined 2026 total.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 15,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease\\\", 2027, due 2027-07-01): Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01?\\nLadder: rung 1 No: NVIDIA has not signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01. / rung 2 Yes: NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by 2027-07-01.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 13 items]\\n[Not shown: 5 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e8 [rumour; moomoo.com, citing The Information; original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] The Information previously reported that NVIDIA was negotiating to guarantee OpenAI’s lease of a large Ohio data center, whose fully built-out cost could reach $500 billion.\\n    Quote: According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\n- e15 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Nvidia announced a partnership program using AI cloud providers to give AI startups access to computing infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday.\\n- e16 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Under Nvidia’s announced partnership program, cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share product and cloud revenue with Nvidia.\\n    Quote: Cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with Nvidia, which is positioning itself as an intermediary helping startups gain direct access to full-stack computing powered by Nvidia chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e10, e24, e25; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e17 [fact; CNBC; original source Sharon AI 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs through the program.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e18 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Firmus Technologies 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, expected to reach 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n- e19 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] NVIDIA is acting as a financial backstop for AI-cloud customers in exchange for a share of their revenue.\\n    Quote: The chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1, e61; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e20 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Under the backstop, NVIDIA agrees to rent back unused GPUs from AI-cloud customers at a fixed rate.\\n    Quote: The backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate.\\n- e21 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com; original source Firmus and Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to adopt NVIDIA's AI-cloud backstop offer.\\n    Quote: Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e24 [party_statement; NVIDIA, in a post quoted by datacenterdynamics.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said its new model lets AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model serving AI-native, enterprise, and ISV customers.\\n    Quote: This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e16; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e25 [party_statement; NVIDIA, in a post quoted by datacenterdynamics.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said the partnerships will generate product revenue plus a share of cloud revenue on supported capacity, creating a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\n    Quote: Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell Nvidia-powered cloud services, with Nvidia earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of Nvidia platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides Nvidia with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e16, e26; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e451 [fact; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies and DayOne ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus Technologies and DayOne are developing NVIDIA's DSX AI Factory Campus in Batam, a planned 360 MW AI data-center complex.\\n    Quote: The Batam development marks Firmus Technologies' first data center investment in Indonesia. The Australian AI infrastructure company is partnering with Singapore-based data center operator DayOne to develop the Nvidia DSX AI Factory Campus, a 360-megawatt AI data center complex that is expected to become one of Southeast Asia's largest AI computing hubs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e46; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e452 [third_party_view; Jakarta Globe, citing The Straits Times; original source The Straits Times ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] The Straits Times projected that the Batam campus could generate up to $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n    Quote: According to The Straits Times , the project is projected to generate as much as $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n- e453 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 to provide AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Quote: Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v11 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus is supplied. The specific report that China plans to permit the three named firms, together with Reuters’ report of progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon o\\n- v12 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much incremental compute capacity will Google, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft add in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 15 to under 20 GW; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: No consensus or company guidance is supplied. The only quantified market expectation shown is Morgan Stanley’s 19.5 GW forecast, which sits in rung 3. That forecast also implies rung 3: 19.5 GW is at least 15 GW but below 20 GW, so it does not cross into rung 4. The 2025 figure is historical context\\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around the reported $650 billion plan; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence does not establish a quantified change to the four companies’ 2026 data-center spending: Meta’s $125–145 billion and Google’s $190 billion figures are total-capex or infrastructure budgets, not the data\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is No (rung 1): no market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or announced commitment specific to OpenAI’s Ohio lease is supplied. The evidence implies Yes by 2027-07-01: the lease-specific report says NVIDIA was negotiating to guarantee the lease, while NVIDIA’s AI-cloud credit-support program provides a relevant, though not equivalent, precedent. The program’s rent-back mechanics and named adopters make that precedent more concrete, but none of the evidence establishes that the Ohio guarantee has been signed; this is a forecast, not a finding that it already holds. The broad AI-capex themes offer some contextual support for infrastructure financing, but do not establish an Ohio-specific commitment.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The evidence makes a signed guarantee by the due date more likely than not, but does not establish that it has been signed. The specific report of negotiations for OpenAI’s Ohio lease is supported directionally by NVIDIA’s subsequently announced credit-support model and evidence that Firmus and Sharon AI adopted it; that precedent is not itself an Ohio-lease guarantee. The general AI-capacity and capex themes add only contextual support.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e8\",\n    \"e15\",\n    \"e19\",\n    \"e20\",\n    \"e21\",\n    \"e24\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e8\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e15\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e16\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e17\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e18\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e19\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e20\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e21\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e24\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e25\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e451\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e452\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e453\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains Yes, but the new evidence adds a concrete NVIDIA credit-support model, its rent-back mechanism, and named adopters. This changes the key supporting facts from a lease-specific negotiation report plus a less concrete precedent; the repeated Ohio report itself adds no new fact.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v12\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v4\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"No market consensus or NVIDIA guidance specific to this guarantee is supplied. The status-quo reference is No (rung 1: no signed guarantee); the reference is not itself a reason to remain there.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Yes by 2027-07-01 (rung 2). No amount or signing date is specified; the forecast follows from reported lease-specific negotiations and the subsequently evidenced credit-support program, with the latter treated as supporting precedent rather than proof of an Ohio commitment.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 16,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v3 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\\\", 2026, due 2026-12-31): Will NVIDIA confirm that first shipments of its Vera Rubin AI chip platform to customers have begun by December 31, 2026, through a press release, earnings call, or quarterly filing?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA confirms that first shipments of Vera Rubin to customers have begun by December 31, 2026. / rung 2 Ruled out: NVIDIA formally announces after July 2, 2026, that it has canceled or abandoned the Vera Rubin platform before any first shipment to a customer. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 32 items]\\n[Not shown: 1 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e40 [fact; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] In an article dated July 2, 2026, Fool.com reported that NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later in 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93, e172; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope / Fool.com; original source Jensen Huang / NVIDIA ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has stated expectations for $1 trillion in orders for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027, according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e72, e312; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e72 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, Fool.com; original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said in March 2026 that he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027, as reported by Fool.com.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e81 [party_statement; Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability at NVIDIA (via bgr.com article by Jonathan Sayers); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a 2026-07-04] NVIDIA, in a company blog post by Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability, announced that NVIDIA DSX AI factories are hosting NVIDIA's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles, using a 100% liquid-cooling design in which coolant enters the chip at 45°C and exits at 55°C.\\n    Quote: In Nvidia's blog post by Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability at Nvidia, it was announced that Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles. … Liquid coolant enters the chip at 45 degrees Celsius, absorbs heat from across the chip's surface, and exits at 55 degrees.\\n- e93 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e97 [fact; Adria Cimino, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia is launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform, per the article's account of Nvidia's plan.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\\n- e99 [party_statement; Adria Cimino, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Nvidia forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e382; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e119 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] NVIDIA's Vera Rubin racks are reported to have first shipments scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the article's account of the launch schedule.\\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\\n- e120 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source  ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into their upcoming products.\\n    Quote: Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI build\\n- e134 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source  ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\n- e168 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, via Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted SemiAnalysis's claims that its next-generation rack architectures are delayed, saying: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e166 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\n- e170 [party_statement; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein, reported by Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (management) ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Hon Hai Precision Industry, one of Nvidia's largest suppliers, reported strong earnings on the Friday before the 2026-07-06 article and its management forecast a continued acceleration in AI demand; Mizuho analyst Jordan Klein called this \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\" No figures for the results were given.\\n    Quote: The analyst additionally pointed to strong earnings last Friday from Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (TW:2317), one of Nvidia's largest suppliers. Klein noted that Hon Hai management forecasted a continued acceleration in AI demand, which the analyst said was \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\"\\n- e172 [party_statement; tikr.com (TIKR); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are set to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud, according to the article.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e180 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA representative told Tom's Hardware, in a statement dated 7/6/2026, that \\\"Our roadmap is intact,\\\" providing no further details in response to follow-up questions about the reported Kyber delay.\\n    Quote: Update 7/6/2026 4:10pm PT : An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e182 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source  ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom's Hardware reported that the delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\\n    Quote: The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\\n- e195 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03 2026-07-07] Nvidia disputed a SemiAnalysis report that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays, according to CNBC; the item reports the dispute occurred on the Monday before the 2026-07-07 article and does not quote Nvidia's statement or give any revised schedule. Nvidia's denial is the company's own assertion that its product plan/schedule stands.\\n    Quote: Similar action took place in Nvidia Monday after the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e182 (); both are in force: Tom's Hardware reported that the delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\\n- e237 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Nvidia is working to release its next-generation Rubin AI chip line-up in the second half of 2026, following the Blackwell generation introduced in late 2024, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e534; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e306 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia denied speculation that its Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure is delayed, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track; the article states Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system.\\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e339, e389; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e134 (); both are in force: NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\\n- e312 [party_statement; Adam Spatacco, Fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management has articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers, as reported by Fool.com on July 9, 2026.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e339 [party_statement; Bloomberg (via moomoo); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Nvidia issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay and confirming that its product roadmap remains intact, a clarification the article says counters concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt its AI competitive lead.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e306; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e251 (); both are in force: Research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks; the article says this could push NVIDIA to use its new credit/revenue-sharing model to sustain demand.\\n- e365 [fact; quantumzeitgeist.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-07; from gnews:b666d35e5247a8ddcc2d7c72 2026-07-10] NVIDIA unveiled Vera, a new data center CPU architected for agentic AI workloads that prioritizes single-threaded (per-core) performance rather than the industry's trend toward higher core counts at the expense of per-core speed. This is a product unveiling, not a confirmed shipment or revenue event.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is challenging a core tenet of modern data center design with Vera, a new CPU built to maximize single-threaded performance at scale, and AI innovator Perplexity is among the first to adopt it. … The newly unveiled NVIDIA Vera CPU is specifically architected to address the demands of agentic AI, a field where maximizing single-threaded performance is paramount\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e386; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e366 [fact; quantumzeitgeist.com; original source Perplexity 2026-07-07; from gnews:b666d35e5247a8ddcc2d7c72 2026-07-10] Perplexity, an AI company developing agentic systems, is among the first to adopt NVIDIA's Vera CPU; after testing it on real coding workflows, Perplexity is planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems.\\n    Quote: Perplexity, a company actively developing agentic systems, is among the first to adopt the NVIDIA Vera CPU, signaling a significant endorsement of the new architecture’s capabilities. … Perplexity is now planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems, demonstrating confidence in its ability to enhance agent performance and overall system efficiency.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e188; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e407 [fact; I/O Fund (io-fund.com article); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2dcfaef1a91c2976a4e347da 2026-07-12] NVIDIA has developed its CMX Context Memory Storage Platform, an SSD-based enclosure connected to Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs over Spectrum-X Ethernet, using 64 BlueField-4 DPUs per rack connected to roughly 9,600 TB of SSD storage capacity; NVIDIA also created the STX reference architecture, a standardized blueprint for storage vendors to build CMX systems that connect to Vera Rubin racks. The I/O Fund reports this as an NVIDIA platform and potential new revenue driver.\\n    Quote: To solve for KV cache bottlenecks, Nvidia has developed its CMX Context Memory Storage Platform. CMX uses an SSD-based enclosure that is connected to Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs over Spectrum-X Ethernet. … The hardware offload engine in CMX is the BlueField-4 DPUs. CMX incorporates 64 BlueField-4 DPUs, which connect to approximately 9,600 TB of SSD storage capacity per rack. … Nvidia also created the\\n- e412 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA (Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] At Nvidia's latest product presentation, CEO Jensen Huang introduced the Vera CPU, a processor designed specifically for AI workloads and agentic AI systems; management says Vera works alongside Nvidia's AI chips and delivers roughly 1.8 times the performance of comparable x86 processors.\\n    Quote: During the company's latest product presentation, CEO Jensen Huang introduced the Vera CPU, a processor designed specifically for AI workloads and agentic AI systems. … Management says Vera delivers roughly 1.8 times the performance of comparable x86 processors while working alongside Nvidia's AI chips.\\n- e413 [rumour; barchart.com (citing unnamed reports); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] According to unnamed reports, Nvidia has begun offering its new Vera processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow. The item frames this as 'reports suggest,' not a confirmed shipment or order.\\n    Quote: Reports also suggest Nvidia has begun offering the new processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow.\\n- e428 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform (reported among its recent product and capability expansions); the item describes it as a launch, not as confirmed customer shipments.\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, released the AI PC platform RTX Spark, expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL), and further invested in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\\n- e429 [rumour; en.sedaily.com, citing unnamed semiconductor-industry sources; original source LG Electronics 2026-07-12; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development in the first half of 2026 of an AI server-rack prototype built to the compute-tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" platform, with the R&D done at a 1.8-megawatt data center cluster at its Pyeongtaek site; LG plans reliability evaluations in the second half and then to secure orders from global big tech companies and begin mass production next year (2027). The report is attributed to unnamed semiconductor-industry sources; it does not state any order, volume or revenue.\\n    Quote: LG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year. … According to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" in the first half of the year. LG plans t\\n- e434 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] In investor-relations discussions, NVIDIA said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\\n    Quote: The company said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e134 (); both are in force: NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\\n- e486 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] According to a report about 21 days before the 2026-07-14 article, South Korea's SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above those on HBM; the article characterizes this as a margin story rather than a demand story.\\n    Quote: The previous big move we wrote about was 21 days ago when the stock dropped 3.7% after a report that South Korea's SK Hynix is slowing its high-bandwidth memory (HBM) expansion rattled the AI-chip complex. … The headline sounds bearish for AI, but the underlying report is a margin story, not a demand story. SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM,\\n- e506 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] The article states that NVIDIA's Blackwell 300 is ramping and that the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it, giving no shipping date for Vera Rubin. It is the author's description of the product roadmap rather than a new NVIDIA announcement.\\n    Quote: Blackwell 300 is ramping, and the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it.\\n- e534 [party_statement; EBC (ebc.com); original source NVIDIA Corp. ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Nvidia's Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, per EBC's report; an analyst cited in the article expects the August earnings report to be a catalyst on margins.\\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e237; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e541 [fact; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Under an eight-year strategic partnership between Firmus Technologies and Nvidia aimed at expanding AI infrastructure across the region, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation GPUs in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies. The plan dates the customer deployment, not confirmed Nvidia shipments or revenue.\\n    Quote: The Batam campus is also part of an eight-year strategic partnership between Firmus Technologies and Nvidia aimed at expanding AI infrastructure across the region. … Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and f\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v4 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's government or a regulator permit NVIDIA to sell H200 AI chips to Chinese customers by 2027-01-31, as shown by an official Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments or revenue to Chinese customers?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; as a market/company reference for this question, that corresponds to rung 3, Still open, rather than a qualifying Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed sale by the deadline. The forecast differs because a \\n- v5 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Commerce Department or Bureau of Industry and Security publicly issue a finding, impose a penalty, or announce a new export-control measure stemming from its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus, U.S. guidance, or stated Commerce/BIS decision deadline is supplied, so the reference remains an unresolved outcome by the due date. The evidence makes a U.S. action more plausible: the investigation is reported, and Singapore charges and broader chip-sm\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much did the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures during calendar 2026, according to their full-year 2026 capex disclosures?: now rung 3 from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). Meta's higher 2026 capex plan, Google's up-to-$190 billion capex guidance, and the report that the big four are planning higher capex all lean upward, but none quantifies th\\n- v8 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?: now rung 3 from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecas\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's reported plan for Vera Rubin systems to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, which sits at rung 1. The full-production report and the more specific Q3/fall shipment schedule make NVIDIA confirmation by year-end the most likely outcome. Reports of delays concern Rubin Ultra/Kyber, while the clarification says the 2026 Rubin GPUs using the Oberon rack are not part of that delay; there is no evidence of a post-July 2 decision abandoning the Vera Rubin platform as a whole. The shipment and confirmation are forecast, not established as already having occurred.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's reported plan for Rubin systems to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, which sits at rung 1. The full-production report, the Q3/fall first-shipment schedule, and the later report that the architecture remains on track for late 2026 make NVIDIA confirmation by year-end the most likely outcome. Reports of delays are either unattributed or concern Rubin Ultra/Kyber; NVIDIA disputed the delay claims, and the clarification says the 2026 Rubin GPUs using the Oberon rack are not part of that reported delay. There is no evidence of a post-July 2 decision abandoning the Vera Rubin platform as a whole, so rung 2 is not supported. The forecast is that shipments and confirmation occur by the deadline, not that they have already been established.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e40\",\n    \"e93\",\n    \"e119\",\n    \"e134\",\n    \"e168\",\n    \"e172\",\n    \"e180\",\n    \"e182\",\n    \"e195\",\n    \"e237\",\n    \"e306\",\n    \"e339\",\n    \"e434\",\n    \"e534\",\n    \"e541\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e40\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e41\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e72\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e81\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e93\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e97\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e99\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e119\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e120\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e134\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e168\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e170\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e172\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e180\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e182\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e195\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e237\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e306\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e312\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e339\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e365\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e366\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e407\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e412\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e413\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e428\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e429\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e434\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e486\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e506\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e534\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e541\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 1, but the reported schedule supporting the forecast is now more specifically described as on track for late 2026, rather than only as a fall-2026 start. The 2027 deployment plan in e541 does not change the forecast for first shipments by the deadline.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v8\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's reported plan is for Rubin systems to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners; that expectation sits at rung 1 (Yes).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"NVIDIA is most likely to confirm first customer shipments by December 31, 2026, with the reported schedule pointing to fall/late 2026. This is supported by reports of full production and Q3/fall first shipments, plus the later report that the schedule remains on track for late 2026; the 2027 customer deployment plan is not evidence that first shipments wait until 2027.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 17,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v7 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\\\", 2028, due 2028-12-31): Will NVIDIA confirm that first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system have begun by December 31, 2028?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By December 31, 2028, NVIDIA confirms that first customer shipments of the Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system have begun. / rung 2 Ruled out: After July 5, 2026 and before December 31, 2028, NVIDIA formally cancels the Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 system or abandons plans to ship it to customers. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2028-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-12-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 32 items]\\n[Not shown: 7 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e90 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to pack 144 GPUs and slated to debut with NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system 'remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.' SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the syst\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e106, e122, e164, e167, e171, e175, e177, e194, e210, e305, e423, e532; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e91 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's 'odd design and heavy operational burden.' SemiAnalysis said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,' which it predicted could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\n    Quote: A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. … \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said. … That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no p\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e107, e165, e169, e178; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e92 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber/Rubin Ultra roadmap had slipped, stating 'Our roadmap is intact.'\\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e108, e124, e168, e180, e195, e211, e252, e424, e533; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e122 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman), reporting SemiAnalysis's claims; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported (in an item published 2026-07-06) that Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release to 2028 from an original 2027 target, because of manufacturing challenges with the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules. SemiAnalysis also said Nvidia's alternative plan — placing two Oberon racks back-to-back to achieve similar power output — was scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back, and that the same manufacturing issues will likely also delay the planned NVL576 system or limit it to small volumes.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported. … The Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027. … However, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the p\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e123 [third_party_view; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman), reporting SemiAnalysis's claims; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis separately reported, about a week before the 2026-07-06 DCD article, that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra chip design in favor of a two-chiplet design, citing manufacturing execution concerns; per SemiAnalysis, the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\n    Quote: The news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e124 [party_statement; DataCenterDynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source Nvidia 2026-07-06; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] In response to a DataCenterDynamics request for comment on SemiAnalysis's Kyber claims, an Nvidia spokesperson said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: In response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e134 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source  ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\n- e164 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com (reporting SemiAnalysis's post on X); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems, specifically the PCB midplane — a central circuit board connecting all the individual components that has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\\n    Quote: Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems. … The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e165 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com (reporting SemiAnalysis); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported additional setbacks to Nvidia's roadmap: a planned alternative design, NVL72x2 — which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back — has been scrapped entirely after cloud providers and large data-center operators pushed back on its unusual form factor and high operational overhead; and the more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has been canceled, leaving only the smaller two-die version that delivers roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis lists more setbacks. A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead. … The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Onl\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e166 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com (reporting SemiAnalysis); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling \\n- e167 [rumour; Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Supply-chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report (reported 2026-07-06) that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 because of manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane, a claim Nvidia disputed.\\n    Quote: Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e168 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, via Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted SemiAnalysis's claims that its next-generation rack architectures are delayed, saying: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e166 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\n- e169 [rumour; Investing.com (uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis's report also alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design after heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients, and that a crucial four-die configuration of a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform had been scrapped.\\n    Quote: The research firm further alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients. … Additionally, the report pointed to a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform, claiming a crucial four-die configuration had been scrapped.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e171 [third_party_view; tikr.com (TIKR); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's next rack-scale server design, Kyber — built to pack 144 Nvidia chips into a single cabinet — has been pushed back more than a year, from 2027 to 2028, due to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board. SemiAnalysis also reported that the larger NVL576 version (eight racks linked optically) is likely to be delayed or ship only in small volumes, and that Nvidia's backup plan of bolting two current-generation racks together was scrapped after cloud customers reportedly rejected it as awkward and expensive to run. SemiAnalysis said this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, potentially giving rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028. … The design was supposed to launch alongside Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia’s next-generation system, in 2027. The holdup comes down to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board inside the system, according to SemiAnalysis. … A larger version, \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e175 [third_party_view; Bloomberg / businesstimes.com.sg; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia's next-generation Kyber NVL144 AI server rack system has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties, specifically setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform. Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment. The report was unconfirmed by Nvidia.\\n    Quote: Asian technology stocks slumped after a report that Nvidia’s next-generation AI server rack system has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties. … Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform. Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment outside of regular office hour\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e177 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting a SemiAnalysis thread on X; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] According to SemiAnalysis, in a thread on X reported by Tom's Hardware, NVIDIA will not ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028 — a delay of more than 12 months — pushing the cabinet intended for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year. The report attributes the holdup to manufacturing challenges with the PCB midplane (which NVIDIA calls the orthogonal backplane) that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches.\\n    Quote: Nvidia reportedly won't ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet meant for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year, according to a SemiAnalysis thread on X. The holdup is ostensibly being caused by manufacturing challenges with a PCB midplane that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches, which Nvidia calls the orthogonal backp\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e178 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack that would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit. The report says no proven alternative remains to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027. … NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running t\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e91; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e179 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics — is likely to slip too, or to ship in low volume, until that optical technology matures.\\n    Quote: NVL576, a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics, is likely to slip too or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures.\\n- e180 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] An NVIDIA representative told Tom's Hardware, in a statement dated 7/6/2026, that \\\"Our roadmap is intact,\\\" providing no further details in response to follow-up questions about the reported Kyber delay.\\n    Quote: Update 7/6/2026 4:10pm PT : An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e181 [rumour; Tom's Hardware (Luke James), reporting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] According to the report, NVIDIA dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part about a week before the 7/6/2026 article, over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\\n    Quote: Nvidia dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part last week over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e123; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e182 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source  ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] Tom's Hardware reported that the delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\\n    Quote: The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\\n- e252 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] An NVIDIA spokesperson said 'Our road map is intact' and that 'Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline,' responding to reported Kyber NVL144 setbacks.\\n    Quote: An NVIDIA spokesperson said, “Our road map is intact.” Further, \\\"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e305 [rumour; barchart.com; original source  ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Unnamed reports suggested that Nvidia's next-generation Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028, against a prior expectation that it would launch in 2027.\\n    Quote: Recently, Nvidia faced reports that suggested its next-generation Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e306 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia denied speculation that its Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure is delayed, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track; the article states Kyber, designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system.\\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e339, e389; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e134 (); both are in force: NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\\n- e319 [party_statement; IEEE Spectrum (Knvul Sheikh); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a4eb2bdfabca94f5887881c6 2026-07-09] Nvidia plans to add even more processors to single interconnected systems, going from 72 GPUs today to as many as 576 by 2027, as it scales its rack-scale systems, according to IEEE Spectrum.\\n    Quote: At the same time, Nvidia is planning to add even more processors to single interconnected systems, going from 72 GPUs today to as many as 576 by 2027.\\n- e339 [party_statement; Bloomberg (via moomoo); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Nvidia issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay and confirming that its product roadmap remains intact, a clarification the article says counters concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt its AI competitive lead.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e306; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e251 (); both are in force: Research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks; the article says this could push NVIDIA to use its new credit/revenue-sharing model to sustain demand.\\n- e389 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corp. ; from gnews:1c3c0c58ed5ec3da43292f9b 2026-07-11] NVIDIA Corp. has denied reports that its Kyber platform — tied to its next-generation AI chips — is being delayed, according to a July 11, 2026 article that frames the denial against reports of a delay to its next generation AI chips.\\n    Quote: AI giant NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) is currently in the news these days due to reports of a delay to its next generation AI chips. … NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) has denied reports that the Kyber platform is being delayed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e306; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e423 [third_party_view; Geoffrey Seiler, fool.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 next-generation AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 because of problems manufacturing a crucial circuit board.\\n    Quote: This has been a key part of CEO Jensen Huang's strategy; however, it could have hit a snag when technology research outfit SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e424 [party_statement; Geoffrey Seiler, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] Nvidia said its roadmap remains intact, responding to the SemiAnalysis report that the Kyber NVL144 next-generation AI rack system could be delayed until 2028, though the article says questions linger.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has come out and said its roadmap remains intact, although questions linger.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e251 (); both are in force: Research firm SemiAnalysis stated in an X post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack system has hit manufacturing setbacks; the article says this could push NVIDIA to use its new credit/revenue-sharing model to sustain demand.\\n- e434 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] In investor-relations discussions, NVIDIA said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\\n    Quote: The company said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e134 (); both are in force: NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\\n- e532 [third_party_view; EBC (ebc.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Industry research firm SemiAnalysis reported over the weekend before the 2026-07-14 article that Nvidia's upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028.\\n    Quote: Over the weekend, reports circulated from industry research firm SemiAnalysis suggesting that Nvidia’s upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e533 [party_statement; EBC (ebc.com); original source NVIDIA Corp. (spokesperson) ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] An Nvidia spokesperson countered SemiAnalysis's report on a Kyber NVL144 delay, saying the company's \\\"roadmap is intact.\\\"\\n    Quote: Although an Nvidia spokesperson quickly countered the reports, telling that the company's \\\"roadmap is intact,\\\" the seeds of doubt were already sown.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v4 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's government or a regulator permit NVIDIA to sell H200 AI chips to Chinese customers by 2027-01-31, as shown by an official Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments or revenue to Chinese customers?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's Q2 FY27 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; as a market/company reference for this question, that corresponds to rung 3, Still open, rather than a qualifying Chinese decision or NVIDIA-confirmed sale by the deadline. The forecast differs because a \\n- v5 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Commerce Department or Bureau of Industry and Security publicly issue a finding, impose a penalty, or announce a new export-control measure stemming from its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus, U.S. guidance, or stated Commerce/BIS decision deadline is supplied, so the reference remains an unresolved outcome by the due date. The evidence makes a U.S. action more plausible: the investigation is reported, and Singapore charges and broader chip-sm\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much did the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures during calendar 2026, according to their full-year 2026 capex disclosures?: now rung 3 from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 700,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). Meta's higher 2026 capex plan, Google's up-to-$190 billion capex guidance, and the report that the big four are planning higher capex all lean upward, but none quantifies th\\n- v8 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) What is the combined calendar-2027 data center capital expenditure of the four major AI hyperscalers, as reported in their full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports?: now rung 3 from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 3; it does not specify spending at or above $1.2 trillion. The evidence still points to spending above $1 trillion, but does not support crossing the $1.2 trillion boundary: Morgan Stanley also forecas\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s planned 2027 launch, which sits at rung 1. The reported push to 2028 weakens the schedule but still places the expected release within the 2028 deadline; NVIDIA’s statements that its roadmap is intact and Rubin Ultra remains on schedule reinforce that first shipments by year-end 2028 remain the most likely outcome. The reported cancellation of a backup design is a risk, not a formal abandonment of Kyber NVL144.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 launch plan, which sits at rung 1. SemiAnalysis’s specific report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 weakens that schedule, but a 2028 launch remains inside the December 31, 2028 deadline; NVIDIA continues to say its roadmap is intact and specifically says Vera Rubin Ultra will scale to NVL576 in its planned 2027 timeline. The reported scrapping of a backup design and the interconnect concerns raise execution risk, but neither is a decision against NVL144 as a whole. Thus rung 1 remains the most likely outcome, though not established as a fact.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e90\",\n    \"e91\",\n    \"e92\",\n    \"e166\",\n    \"e179\",\n    \"e182\",\n    \"e252\",\n    \"e306\",\n    \"e319\",\n    \"e434\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e90\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e91\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e92\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e122\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e123\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e124\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e134\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e164\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e165\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e166\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e167\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e168\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e169\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e171\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e175\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e177\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e178\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e179\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e180\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e181\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e182\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e252\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e305\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e306\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e319\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e339\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e389\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e423\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e424\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e434\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e532\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e533\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and key implied timing remain consistent with the previous conclusion: the reported delay points to 2028, still within the deadline, while NVIDIA’s roadmap statements support continued execution. The newer statements do not change the implied NVL144 shipment window.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v8\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA’s stated 2027 launch plan for Kyber is the reference and sits at rung 1 (Yes).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely timing is first customer shipments in 2028: SemiAnalysis reports a delay of more than 12 months from the 2027 target, placing release in 2028, which remains before the December 31, 2028 deadline. NVIDIA’s stated plan for NVL576 in 2027 is supportive but is not itself confirmation of NVL144 shipments.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 18,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v10 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA’s proposed investment in Firmus Technologies\\\", 2026, due 2026-12-31): Will NVIDIA complete its proposed investment in Firmus Technologies’ preference-share raising by 2026-12-31?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA completes the proposed preference-share investment by 2026-12-31. / rung 2 No: NVIDIA does not complete the proposed preference-share investment by 2026-12-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 1 items]\\n- e172 [rumour; afr.com (Street Talk); original source afr.com (Street Talk) 2026-07-08; from gnews:91a2f447977f812557cf0f6c 2026-07-08] NVIDIA reportedly indicated it would invest about US$500 million in Firmus Technologies preference shares, which are designed to convert to ordinary equity at Firmus’ IPO later in 2026.\\n    Quote: Street Talk can reveal that the $US4.9 trillion chip developer has put up its hand for about $US500 million ($721 million) worth of Firmus’ preference shares, which are designed to convert to ordinary equity at the initial public offering later this year.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v11 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy NVIDIA H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Beijing’s reported restriction on Chinese firms purchasing H200s, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus is supplied. The specific report that China plans to permit the three named firms, together with Reuters’ report of progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon o\\n- v12 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much incremental compute capacity will Google, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft add in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 15 to under 20 GW; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: No consensus or company guidance is supplied. The only quantified market expectation shown is Morgan Stanley’s 19.5 GW forecast, which sits in rung 3. That forecast also implies rung 3: 19.5 GW is at least 15 GW but below 20 GW, so it does not cross into rung 4. The 2025 figure is historical context\\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around the reported $650 billion plan; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion, which sits in rung 3. The new evidence does not establish a quantified change to the four companies’ 2026 data-center spending: Meta’s $125–145 billion and Google’s $190 billion figures are total-capex or infrastructure budgets, not the data\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 Neutral comparison: around NVIDIA's reported $1 trillion projection; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $1 trillion projection, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies 2027 spending above $1 trillion, but gives no quantified basis for expecting at least $1.1 trillion; the most likely outcome therefore remains within rung 3 rather than crossing into rung 4.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s reported proposed investment, which points to completion by the deadline and sits at rung 1. The specific report that NVIDIA indicated it would invest about US$500 million in preference shares designed to convert at Firmus’ IPO later in 2026 keeps completion more likely than non-completion; the report is unconfirmed, but lack of confirmation alone does not establish that the investment will fail to complete.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s proposed preference-share investment, which points to completion by the deadline and sits at rung 1. The specific report that NVIDIA indicated it would invest about US$500 million, with the shares converting at Firmus’ IPO later in 2026, supports completion before year-end. The report is not a confirmed closing, but that uncertainty does not make non-completion the more likely outcome; the forecast remains at rung 1.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e172\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e172\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and its supporting key fact remain the same as the previous conclusion; the evidence in view repeats the specific reported investment indication.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA’s proposed investment points to completion by 2026-12-31 and sits at rung 1. No separate market consensus is supplied.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely outcome is completion by 2026-12-31 of the reported roughly US$500 million preference-share investment, intended to convert at Firmus’ IPO later in 2026. This is inferred from the reported investment indication and timing, not from confirmation that the transaction has closed.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 19,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue growth be in FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 100%. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 75% but below 100%. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 50% but below 75%. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 25% but below 50%. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 total revenue growth is below 25%.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 184 items]\\n[Not shown: 104 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e42 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Wall Street analysts project NVIDIA revenue growth of 82% in FY2027 and 41% in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e188; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e54 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis projected NVIDIA data-center compute revenue in the second half of FY2027 would be 20% above Wall Street consensus.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e55 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] NVIDIA plans to launch its first stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\\n- e59 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis estimates NVIDIA’s second-half 2026 shipment volumes and revenue above general-market estimates, based on its supply-chain tracking.\\n    Quote: Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\\n- e101 [fact; 247WallSt author Omor Ibne Ehsan; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, and data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\\n    Quote: Q1 FY27 revenue came in at $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84, e131, e197, e370, e373; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e102 [party_statement; 247WallSt author Omor Ibne Ehsan; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91.0 billion and non-GAAP gross margin of 75%.\\n    Quote: Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e225, e264, e275, e283, e379; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e104 [third_party_view; 247WallSt author Omor Ibne Ehsan; original source Omor Ibne Ehsan 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author estimates that hyperscalers account for about 50% of NVIDIA's data center revenue and are funding Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA alternatives.\\n    Quote: About 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\\n- e108 [third_party_view; 247WallSt author Omor Ibne Ehsan; original source Omor Ibne Ehsan 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author cites $119 billion of NVIDIA supply commitments and says they increase demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow.\\n    Quote: Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e194, e274; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e109 [third_party_view; 247WallSt author Omor Ibne Ehsan; original source Omor Ibne Ehsan 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author estimates that six companies generate roughly half of NVIDIA data center revenue and are engineering alternatives to reduce purchases from NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The numbers also do not argue for aggressively adding. Roughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA’s price list\\n- e127 [third_party_view; Investing.com, citing analysts quoted by Bloomberg; original source Analysts cited by Bloomberg ; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] Analysts cited by Bloomberg said the reported technical setbacks could constrain NVIDIA's future hardware scale-up capacity and give AMD's MI500X and Broadcom-backed custom TPUs a wider competitive window.\\n    Quote: According to analysts cited by Bloomberg, these rumored technical setbacks could severely cap the scale-up capacity of Nvidia's future hardware. This perceived vulnerability may create a wider competitive window for alternative AI platforms, specifically AMD's MI500X and Broadcom-backed custom TPUs.\\n- e130 [third_party_view; tikr.com (analyst); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis expects NVIDIA data-center compute revenue in the second half of FY2027 to be 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e54; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e134 [third_party_view; The Business Times, reporting Bloomberg Intelligence’s survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg Intelligence’s survey of Chinese executives said they expect domestic products to receive 46% of AI-accelerator budgets over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months. This is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e163; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [third_party_view; The Business Times, reporting Bloomberg Intelligence’s survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg Intelligence’s survey expected Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips became harder to find and local firms took over; the survey cited Beijing’s urging that Chinese technology firms not use H20 chips.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e192; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e145 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet ; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house processors, increasing competition from cloud providers’ specialized AI chips.\\n    Quote: The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e260; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e162 [third_party_view; Reuters, as reported by mlq.ai; original source Analyst Richard Windey ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Analyst Richard Windey said U.S. export rules have effectively eliminated Nvidia's China revenue and limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China.\\n    Quote: Analyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e106, e167; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e163 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Industry surveys, as reported by mlq.ai ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to allocate 46% of AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, versus roughly 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134, e189, e347; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e173 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b40db2c507df72ec016e99e6 2026-07-08] NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of more than $215.9 billion, up 65% year over year.\\n    Quote: The company's most recent fiscal 2026 revenue topped $215.9 billion, up 65% year over year and well ahead of the $11.7 billion it posted in 2019.\\n- e174 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Analysts, as reported by finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:b40db2c507df72ec016e99e6 2026-07-08] Analysts forecast NVIDIA FY2027 revenue of $392.7 billion.\\n    Quote: And analysts are anticipating further gains of $392.7 billion in its current fiscal year.\\n- e175 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:79cc3f345545cdd3d37c0472 2026-07-08] NVIDIA’s revenue grew 74.6% and net income grew 120.7% in its most recent quarter.\\n    Quote: In the most recent quarter alone, Nvidia increased its revenue by 74.6% and its net income by a jaw-dropping 120.7%.\\n- e188 [third_party_view; tradingview.com; original source Bloomberg-tracked analysts ; from gnews:a09988fd9c5c1a739a14f873 2026-07-08] Bloomberg-tracked analysts project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82% year over year.\\n    Quote: The company is projected to generate $228 billion in profit on $393 billion in sales in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31, representing expected growth of 90% and 82%, respectively\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42, e259; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e189 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source Chinese executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Chinese executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they expect domestic products to receive 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e163; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e192 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence survey expects Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips become harder to find and local firms take over; Beijing has urged Chinese technology firms not to use H20 chips.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e196 [third_party_view; Investing.com, reporting Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya’s note; original source Vivek Arya ; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya reported that NVIDIA sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capital-expenditure growth.\\n    Quote: Sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e205; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e213 [third_party_view; TechCrunch author Russell Brandom; original source TechCrunch author Russell Brandom 2026-07-09; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] The author reports that memory companies increased high-bandwidth memory chip prices tenfold over the past year.\\n    Quote: they have been able to increase prices tenfold over the past year.\\n- e215 [third_party_view; TechCrunch author Russell Brandom; original source TechCrunch author Russell Brandom 2026-07-09; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] The author reports that the spot price for an hour of Nvidia H100 GPU compute peaked at about $3.20 in May 2026 and then steadily declined.\\n    Quote: Just like Nvidia’s stock price, there’s a peak in May (around $3.20 an hour) and then a steady drop-off.\\n- e216 [third_party_view; TechCrunch author Russell Brandom; original source TechCrunch author Russell Brandom 2026-07-09; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] The author reports that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and OpenAI have launched custom processors to reduce dependence on Nvidia, and says those chips are good enough to drive down compute prices.\\n    Quote: Google , Amazon , Microsoft , and even OpenAI have launched their own custom processors to lessen their dependence on Nvidia; even if those chips aren’t as good as the latest model from Nvidia, they’re good enough to drive down the price of compute.\\n- e218 [third_party_view; Citi analyst Atif Malik, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com; original source Citi analyst Atif Malik ; from gnews:86c243945145ed901feb26cf 2026-07-09] Citi analyst Atif Malik said NVIDIA had strong access to DRAM memory amid constrained industry supply.\\n    Quote: We maintain Nvidia as our top buy-rated mega-cap data center semis pick on strong access to DRAM memory amidst constrained industry supply,\\n- e222 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source Unnamed experts cited by barchart.com ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Experts cited by barchart.com say DeepSeek's chip is unlikely to materially affect NVIDIA's near-term revenue, partly because China contributes less to sales, but custom silicon could gradually erode NVIDIA's pricing power and margins.\\n    Quote: Although experts note that DeepSeek's move is unlikely to materially impact Nvidia's near-term revenue, particularly given China's shrinking contribution to Nvidia's sales, it reinforces concerns that custom silicon could gradually erode the company's pricing power and margins.\\n- e225 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-05-20; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] NVIDIA forecast Q2 FY2027 revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%.\\n    Quote: Additionally, Nvidia issued another optimistic outlook for the second quarter of fiscal 2027, forecasting revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e102, e336; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e226 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-05-20; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 guidance assumed no China Data Center compute revenue because of U.S. export restrictions.\\n    Quote: The guidance assumes no contribution from China Data Center compute revenue due to ongoing U.S. export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e284; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\