il 2030.\\n    Quote: SK Group chairman Chey Tae-won said in June that the company plans to ramp up its memory chip capacity over the next five years to address a shortage that could last until 2030, Bloomberg reports .\\n- e289 [third_party_view; Thomas Richmond, 24/7 Wall St., quoting Kristina Partsinevelos; original source Counterpoint Research ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Kristina Partsinevelos cited Counterpoint Research figures that SK Hynix held roughly 58% of the high-bandwidth memory market, while Micron and Samsung held roughly 21% each.\\n    Quote: “SK Hynix is the world leader in high bandwidth memory . The memory that feeds AI chips with roughly 58% of that market. Micron and Samsung competitors split roughly 21% each … as per Counterpoint Research.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e339; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e290 [third_party_view; Thomas Richmond, 24/7 Wall St., reporting Kristina Partsinevelos's CNBC segment; original source Kristina Partsinevelos 2026-07-10; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Kristina Partsinevelos said SK Hynix revenue was expected to triple to $235 billion in 2026.\\n    Quote: Partsinevelos noted revenue is expected to triple to $235 billion this year\\n- e291 [third_party_view; Thomas Richmond, 24/7 Wall St., reporting Kristina Partsinevelos's CNBC segment; original source Kristina Partsinevelos 2026-07-10; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Kristina Partsinevelos said SK Hynix had committed to more than $720 billion of capital investment over the coming years, primarily in South Korea.\\n    Quote: with the company committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years, primarily in South Korea.\\n- e292 [fact; Thomas Richmond, 24/7 Wall St.; original source U.S. CHIPS Act ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] About $458 million in U.S. CHIPS Act support was earmarked for SK Hynix's Indiana advanced packaging facility.\\n    Quote: A separate roughly $458 million from the U.S. CHIPS Act is earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility.\\n- e293 [third_party_view; Thomas Richmond, 24/7 Wall St., quoting Kristina Partsinevelos; original source Kristina Partsinevelos 2026-07-10; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Kristina Partsinevelos forecast that new SK Hynix supply would not arrive before late 2027, prolonging memory shortages and record-high prices.\\n    Quote: “None of that supply, though, arrives before late 2027, which keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now,” Partsinevelos said.\\n- e294 [fact; Thomas Richmond, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Micron reported Q3 FY2026 revenue of $41.456 billion, up 345.7% year over year; non-GAAP diluted EPS was $25.11 and GAAP gross margin was 84.6%.\\n    Quote: Micron posted Q3 FY2026 revenue of $41.456 billion, up 345.7% year over year, with non-GAAP diluted EPS of $25.11 and GAAP gross margin of 84.6%.\\n- e295 [party_statement; Thomas Richmond, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Micron guided to revenue of $49.0 billion to $51.0 billion for the following quarter.\\n    Quote: Guidance for the following quarter calls for revenue of $50.0 billion plus or minus $1.0 billion.\\n- e296 [party_statement; Thomas Richmond, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Micron said its HBM4 was in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform and that HBM4E was in development, with volume production expected in 2027.\\n    Quote: Micron’s HBM4, built on 1-beta DRAM, is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform, and HBM4E is in development, with volume production expected in calendar 2027.\\n- e297 [fact; Thomas Richmond, 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year; Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments were $119.0 billion.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e298 [rumour; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com, reporting unnamed rumors ; from gnews:170f44b2067e0405a5f5528a 2026-07-10] Recent rumors moved NVIDIA’s RTX 50 SUPER refresh debut from 2026 expectations to possibly late 2026 or CES 2027.\\n    Quote: Recent rumors have moved the refresh from 2026 expectations toward a possible late 2026 or CES 2027 debut\\n- e299 [rumour; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com, reporting an unnamed latest report ; from gnews:170f44b2067e0405a5f5528a 2026-07-10] An unnamed latest report said RTX 50 SUPER products were prepared and at least one board partner had received cards, but NVIDIA had put the lineup on hold while evaluating 3 GB GDDR7 memory pricing; no revised launch schedule was reported.\\n    Quote: the products are already prepared and that at least one board partner has received RTX 50 SUPER cards, but NVIDIA has placed them on hold while it evaluates the pricing of 3 GB GDDR7 memory. No revised launch schedule has reportedly been provided\\n- e300 [rumour; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com, reporting unnamed sources ; from gnews:170f44b2067e0405a5f5528a 2026-07-10] The article reported that 3 GB GDDR7 modules for the RTX 5070 Ti SUPER’s 24 GB configuration cost an estimated $60–$70 each, versus around $20 for current 2 GB modules.\\n    Quote: the 3 GB GDDR7 modules required for its 24 GB configuration reportedly cost between $60 and $70 each, compared to around $20 for current 2 GB modules\\n- e301 [party_statement; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA estimates that supporting frontier AI models accounts for about 20% of its business; the item gives no period or revenue measure.\\n    Quote: To put some numbers behind that, Nvidia estimates about 20% of its business comes from supporting frontier AI models built by companies like OpenAI and Anthropic.\\n- e302 [third_party_view; tikr.com; original source TD Cowen ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] TD Cowen estimates that enterprise applications across industries account for a low-to-mid-teens share of NVIDIA revenue; the item gives no period.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, TD Cowen estimates that Nvidia’s revenue from enterprise applications across multiple industries is in the low-to-mid-teens range.\\n- e303 [third_party_view; tikr.com; original source TD Cowen ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] TD Cowen reported that rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices, citing tight computing capacity.\\n    Quote: Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices.\\n- e304 [fact; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $82 billion, up 85% year over year, for an unspecified recent quarter; the company said this was its third consecutive quarter of accelerating growth.\\n    Quote: The company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, marking its third straight quarter of accelerating growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e131; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e305 [fact; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported data-center revenue of $75 billion, up 92% year over year, for an unspecified recent quarter.\\n    Quote: Data center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e89, e366; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e307 [third_party_view; tikr.com; original source Mark Zuckerberg ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg said progress in AI agents was slower than expected.\\n    Quote: Comments from Meta’s Mark Zuckerberg about slower-than-expected progress in AI agents have also added caution to the broader AI narrative.\\n- e308 [third_party_view; tikr.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] Bank of America views the recent semiconductor-sector pullback as a temporary adjustment, not a sign that AI demand is fading.\\n    Quote: Still, firms like Bank of America view the recent semiconductor sector pullback as a temporary adjustment rather than a sign that AI demand is fading, keeping names like Nvidia stock in focus for investors watching this space closely.\\n- e309 [third_party_view; es.tradingview.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:1bfc0e875ab1f26dcf48fb06 2026-07-11] Morgan Stanley said AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of Nvidia’s demand, with enterprise AI adoption expanding beyond hyperscale cloud providers.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley reaffirmed its over-weight rating after meeting with Nvidia’s management, arguing that the AI infrastructure cycle remains in its early innings despite quarterly revenue approaching the $100 billion milestone. The bank noted that AI laboratories now account for roughly 20% of Nvidia’s demand, highlighting how enterprise AI adoption is expanding beyond hyperscale cloud providers.\\n- e310 [third_party_view; es.tradingview.com; original source TD Cowen ; from gnews:1bfc0e875ab1f26dcf48fb06 2026-07-11] TD Cowen said hyperscaler capital spending remained resilient and demand for Nvidia’s AI platforms continued.\\n    Quote: TD Cowen maintained a similarly constructive outlook, citing resilient capital spending from hyperscalers and continued demand for Nvidia’s AI platforms.\\n- e313 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Nvidia revenue increased 85% year over year during its past quarter.\\n    Quote: During its past quarter, Nvidia's revenue increased at an 85% clip.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e88; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e314 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Wall Street analysts expect Nvidia revenue to grow 96% year over year in Q2; the item does not specify which Q2.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts expect 96% growth during Q2.\\n- e317 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool; original source Keithen Drury 2026-07-12; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] The author says Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta, Anthropic, and OpenAI in late 2026 and into 2027, with new clients launching chips next year.\\n    Quote: Broadcom expects major growth next year as new clients launch their custom AI chips. Currently, Broadcom's major customer is Alphabet , with its Tensor Processing Units (TPUs). However, Broadcom is also making custom AI chips for Meta Platforms , Anthropic, and OpenAI, and production for those chips is scheduled to ramp up late this year and into next.\\n- e318 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool; original source Keithen Drury 2026-07-12; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] The author says NVIDIA is growing faster than AMD and Broadcom, and that market expectations have NVIDIA maintaining its growth lead for the full fiscal year while Broadcom narrows the gap.\\n    Quote: From a growth standpoint, Nvidia still stands alone at the top, growing far faster than the other two.\\nLooking ahead, the market still expects Nvidia to maintain its lead for the full fiscal year, though Broadcom is starting to close the gap.\\n- e319 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source The Information ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reportedly shelved the RTX 50 Super gaming GPU refresh, including the completed-design 24GB RTX 5080 Super, in December 2025.\\n    Quote: Nvidia quietly shelved its planned RTX 50 “Super” refresh back in December 2025 — including a 24GB RTX 5080 Super that had reportedly reached completed-design stage\\n- e320 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org; original source tech-insider.org 2026-07-12; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA had shipped no new gaming GPUs in 2026, and the report said none were planned for the rest of the year.\\n    Quote: the company has shipped zero new gaming GPUs all year and has nothing left on the calendar to change that\\n- e321 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source The Information ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA's next-generation RTX 60-series gaming GPUs were reportedly pushed back to 2028 from an earlier rumored late-2027 launch.\\n    Quote: company’s next-generation RTX 60-series remains pushed back to 2028.\\n- e322 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source tech-insider.org ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reportedly planned to cut RTX 50-series gaming GPU production by an estimated 15%–20% through at least Q3 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is reportedly cutting production of existing RTX 50-series cards already on shelves by an estimated 15% to 20% through at least Q3 2026\\n- e323 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source Unnamed supply-chain sources ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA's RTX 5060 was reportedly pulled from retail for at least six months.\\n    Quote: The entry-level RTX 5060 has taken the hardest hit, reportedly pulled from retail entirely for at least six months.\\n- e324 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source CNBC ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron reportedly sold out their 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, according to CNBC.\\n    Quote: Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron can produce it, and all three manufacturers have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, according to CNBC’s reporting from January 2026.\\n- e325 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org; original source Unnamed source cited by tech-insider.org ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] HBM demand was projected to grow about 70% year over year in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: HBM demand is projected to grow roughly 70% year-over-year in 2026\\n- e326 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org; original source Unnamed source cited by tech-insider.org ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Producing a bit of HBM was estimated to use three times the wafer capacity required for a bit of standard DDR5.\\n    Quote: producing a single bit of HBM consumes an estimated three times the wafer capacity of standard DDR5\\n- e327 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org; original source Micron and Kearney’s PERLab ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Micron suggested memory supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027 and could return gradually to balance by 2028; Kearney's PERLab reportedly projected the shortage could last into 2030.\\n    Quote: Micron has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028, while other forecasters take a bleaker view: an analysis from Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030 .\\n- e328 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org; original source PCWorld ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] PCWorld reporting cited by the article put N